insert into public.academy_course_structures
    (app_slug, tier, structure, content_version)
insert into public.academy_quiz_questions
    (app_slug, tier, scope, module_key, ord, prompt, options, answer_index, explanation)
values
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-heterogeneity', 1, 'Two 84 foot columns share an arithmetic average permeability of 250 md. One is uniform rock; the other is five layers spanning roughly 100 to 600 md. What does the shared average fail to carry?', '["That the layered column holds more oil in place, because porosity rises with permeability across the five beds.", "That the layered column has the lower total injectivity, so it needs longer to reach any given injected volume.", "That the layered column starts making water while most of its oil still sits behind fronts that have not arrived.", "That the layered column should be averaged harmonically rather than arithmetically, which changes the 250 md figure."]'::jsonb, 2, 'For the same pressure gradient the front velocity in a layer goes as its permeability, so the fast layer arrives at the producer while the slow layers are still part way along. The uniform column sweeps everything before any water is produced. Nothing in the comparison says anything about porosity, and the layered column is if anything the more injective; the averaging rule is not what is at stake.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-heterogeneity', 2, 'In the layered-sweep methods, what exactly is the vertical sweep EV at a given moment?', '["The thickness-weighted mean of each layer''s fractional front distance, with breakthrough at 1.", "The fraction of layers whose fronts have reached the producer, each layer counted once regardless of its thickness.", "The fraction of the injected pore volume that has entered layers other than the fastest one.", "The ratio of the swept permeability-thickness to the total permeability-thickness of the column."]'::jsonb, 0, 'EV is sum(h_j x_j) / sum(h_j): every layer contributes its own thickness times how far its front has travelled, and a layer that has broken through contributes its full thickness. Counting layers rather than thickness would make a thin streak worth as much as a thick interval. Weighting by kh would be a flow-capacity fraction, which is a different quantity.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-heterogeneity', 3, 'The Dykstra-Parsons coefficient is defined as V = (k50 - k84.1) / k50. What is k84.1?', '["The permeability exceeded by 84.1 percent of the samples, read directly off the raw data.", "The permeability one standard deviation below the median on the fitted log-probability line.", "The 84.1st percentile of the permeability distribution, so the value only 15.9 percent of layers exceed.", "The arithmetic mean permeability, which for a log-normal distribution falls at the 84.1 percent point."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The definition is read off the FITTED line, not the raw sample, and it is the value one sigma below the median in log space. That is why the whole computation reduces to fitting sigma. The other readings put it above the median or confuse it with the mean, which for a log-normal sits above the median rather than at a fixed percentile.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-heterogeneity', 4, 'The engine computes V as 1 - exp(-sigma) rather than by reading two permeabilities off a plot. What does that shortcut assume?', '["That the layers are sorted into descending permeability order before sigma is fitted.", "That at least three positive permeabilities were supplied, which is the engine''s own minimum.", "That the plotting position used is (i + 0.5) / n rather than one of the competing conventions.", "That the permeability distribution is log-normal, so that k84.1 = k50 exp(-sigma) holds."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The identity k84.1 = k50 exp(-sigma) is a property of the log-normal, and everything else in the reduction follows from it. Sorting, the sample minimum and the plotting position are all real parts of the implementation, but the algebraic shortcut itself stands or falls on log-normality alone. On a bimodal sand the fitted line runs between two populations and V describes neither.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-heterogeneity', 5, 'The engine assigns each sorted permeability the plotting position (i + 0.5) / n on a zero-based index. Why does that choice matter more on five layers than on five hundred?', '["The competing conventions differ mostly at the tails, which on a small sample is where the extreme values sit.", "Smaller samples are fitted with a different regression kernel, which is more sensitive to the position.", "The Acklam approximation loses accuracy below about ten points, so the quantiles themselves become unreliable.", "With few layers the engine falls back to the median pair rather than a least squares fit, so only the ends are used."]'::jsonb, 0, 'i/n, (i - 0.5)/n and i/(n+1) agree in the middle of the distribution and separate at the ends. A five-layer set has two of its five points in those tails, so the fitted slope moves and V can differ in the second decimal. The kernel, the approximation''s accuracy and the fitting method do not change with sample size. Quote the plotting position with a V from a small sample.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-heterogeneity', 6, 'The exact standard normal quantile of 0.841344746068543 is 1 by construction. The engine''s inverse normal returns 1.000000000927422. What should you conclude?', '["The plotting position is misaligned by half a rank, which shows up as a systematic bias in the ninth decimal of every quantile the engine returns.", "The least squares intercept has accumulated rounding, which propagates back into the quantile.", "The Acklam approximation is performing exactly to the absolute error band it states, below 1.15e-9.", "The probability argument was rounded on the way in, so the input rather than the function carries the error."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Acklam''s rational approximation states an absolute error below 1.15e-9 and the discrepancy sits in the ninth decimal, exactly there. It is not a symptom of anything else in the chain, and it is far below anything that matters for a permeability fit. Having looked at it once tells you how much precision a V from this engine can carry.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-heterogeneity', 7, 'Handed two positive permeabilities, the engine returns the error ''Need at least 3 positive permeabilities.'' What is the statistical reason for that floor?', '["Two points cannot be assigned distinct plotting positions under the (i + 0.5) / n convention, which collapses both of them onto the median.", "A line through two points fits perfectly, so no residual information about sigma survives.", "The inverse normal is undefined at the two probabilities a sample of two would produce.", "The engine needs a median value, and with an even count there is no sample point at the median."]'::jsonb, 1, 'With two points the regression is exact by construction and the fit has no degrees of freedom left, so the sigma it reports is not an estimate of anything. The plotting positions 0.25 and 0.75 are perfectly distinct and the quantile function is defined at both. Three is a floor rather than a recommendation: a V from three layers is a two-parameter fit to three points.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-heterogeneity', 8, 'Five identical permeabilities are fed to the engine and it reports V = 2.220446049250313e-16 rather than 0. What is the right handling?', '["Re-run the fit at a tighter convergence tolerance, which will drive the value to a hard zero.", "Treat the value as a genuine trace of heterogeneity that the sample was too small to resolve.", "Flag it as a defect, because a homogeneous set must return exactly zero from a correct implementation.", "Report V as 0, record the raw double, and treat the tail as least squares rounding."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The value is machine epsilon out of the regression arithmetic on a set that carries no spread at all. The engine already clamps small negative sigma to zero so that float noise cannot produce a negative variation, which is the same concern handled at the other sign. There is no tolerance to tighten and no heterogeneity to resolve: the input is exactly uniform.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-heterogeneity', 9, 'The Ekene layer permeabilities were built as k_i = 250 exp(-ln2 z_i), where z_i is the quantile of the engine''s own plotting position. What does the engine''s clean recovery of V = 0.5 actually establish?', '["That the log-normal model is an adequate description of permeability distributions in real sands.", "That five layers are enough to estimate V reliably, since the estimate came back exact.", "That the code, the plotting position and the arithmetic all match what the design assumed.", "That the fitted line is a good description of the data, which the V value on its own confirms."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The data were placed ON the fitted line, so the fit reproduces the line. That is a validation of the method against itself, and what it proves is that the code, the convention and the arithmetic carry no bias. It says nothing about real rock, nothing about sample sufficiency, and a single V value never tells you whether points fall on a line. For that you look at the plot.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-heterogeneity', 10, 'The design value is exactly 250 md and the fit returns k50 = 250.0000000000001. Where does the last figure come from?', '["k50 is the exponential of the regression intercept, and neither the intercept sum nor exp(ln 250) is exact in binary.", "The plotting position places no layer exactly at the median, so k50 is interpolated between the two nearest.", "The Acklam quantile at 0.5 returns a value just off zero, which shifts the fitted line at the median.", "The layer permeabilities were stored rounded to sixteen figures, and the fit propagates that rounding."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The intercept is a sum of products of quantiles and logarithms, each rounded to a double, and exponentiating it back is a second inexact step. One ulp is what survives. The plotting position (2 + 0.5)/5 is exactly 0.5 and the quantile there is exactly 0, so the median layer is on the grid. Report 250, record the raw double, and never key an assessment on the trailing digit.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-heterogeneity', 11, 'What is the cost of learning a method on a planted dataset like the Ekene column?', '["It cannot expose implementation errors, because the answer was chosen to match the implementation.", "It teaches nothing about how messy real data is, where log-normality is the assumption under test.", "It fixes V at a value too low to be representative, since real sands rarely fall below 0.8.", "It makes the fitted line depend on the plotting position, which a real dataset would not."]'::jsonb, 1, 'On a planted set the fit reproduces the plant, so outliers, curvature and the compromise line never appear, and those are exactly what real analysis is about. It does expose implementation errors, which is its point: on a real set with an unknown answer a coding error and an oddity in the rock look identical. V = 0.5 is squarely typical, and the plotting position matters on any small sample.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-heterogeneity', 12, 'The Ekene column is supplied in depth order with thicknesses 18, 22, 16, 14, 14. What does the stage table''s first row describe?', '["The 18 foot layer at the top of the sand, since normalisation preserves the order it was given in.", "The 14 foot layer at the base, because water slumps to the bottom of the interval first.", "The 16 foot layer at 250 md, the median permeability, which the engine takes as its reference stage.", "The 22 foot layer at 607.7507038307907 md, which sits second from the top of the sand."]'::jsonb, 3, 'normalizeLayers sorts by permeability descending, so the thickness column comes out as 22, 14, 16, 18, 14 and the first stage is the most permeable layer wherever it happens to sit. Here that is L2, second from the top. Depth enters these methods nowhere: gravity segregation is a real mechanism but neither Dykstra-Parsons nor Stiles models it.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-heterogeneity', 13, 'An engineer reads a stage table row as ''the first layer'' and plans to shut off the top of the sand. What has gone wrong?', '["Stage index counts permeability order, and the engine carries no input label through to the row.", "The stage table is indexed from zero, so the first row is actually the second layer down the column.", "The stage table reports broken-through layers only, so the top layer is absent from it entirely.", "The stage table is built after gravity segregation is applied, which reorders the column by density."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Any routine that sorts its input silently produces output indices that no longer refer to your input indices, and this one carries no key through. The quickest check is thickness: exactly one input layer is 22 feet, which locates the first stage as L2. The indexing base, the row coverage and gravity are not the issue. Say ''the 607 md interval'' or give its depth.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-heterogeneity', 14, 'Two columns are identical except that one carries its 600 md rock as a thin streak and the other as a thick interval. What do they share and what separates them?', '["They share both V and the coverage at first breakthrough, because thickness cancels out of both.", "They differ in V, because the fit weights each permeability by the thickness it represents.", "They share V, because only the permeability values enter it, and the stage coverages separate them.", "They differ in V and share the coverages, because coverage is a permeability ratio at each stage."]'::jsonb, 2, 'V is computed from the k column alone, so the two columns return the same value even though a thin thief zone carries very little volume and a thick one carries most of the flood. The stage tables do use thickness, so the coverage numbers do distinguish them. This is why V belongs in a layered analysis as an input rather than as its summary.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-heterogeneity', 15, 'On the Ekene column, coverage at first breakthrough is 0.582878889525386 at M = 0.5 and 0.44357724095944917 at M = 5.0, against V = 0.5. What follows?', '["V is only valid near a mobility ratio of 1, and should be refitted whenever M moves away from it.", "Coverage depends on the fluids as well as the rock, so the closeness of V and coverage here is an accident.", "Coverage brackets V from both sides, so V can be read as the coverage at the field''s own mobility ratio.", "The spread shows the log-normal fit is poor, since a good fit would hold coverage near V."]'::jsonb, 1, 'V is a rock property and the mobility ratio is a fluid property; the two are independent inputs to the sweep. Reading ''V = 0.5 means about half the column is swept at breakthrough'' builds an intuition on a coincidence of this column at this M. V is not refitted against M, it does not bracket anything, and the quality of the log-normal fit is judged on the plot rather than on where coverage lands.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-layered-sweep', 1, 'Both layered sweep methods assume no crossflow between layers. Which way does that assumption bias the predicted sweep?', '["Optimistically, because water confined to its own layer reaches the producer without being lost to adjacent beds.", "Neither way, because crossflow redistributes water between layers without changing the total volume injected.", "Optimistically at unfavourable mobility ratios and pessimistically at favourable ones, following the sign of M minus 1.", "Pessimistically: real crossflow slows the fast layer and speeds the slow one, so the true column sweeps better than the isolated-layer prediction."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Water leaving a fast high-pressure layer for its slower neighbour retards the leading front and advances the lagging one, so the real column sweeps better than the isolated-layer prediction. That makes these methods conservative for vertical conformance, which is a reasonable place for a screening tool to sit. The bias does not depend on M, and it is a change in sweep rather than in injected volume.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-layered-sweep', 2, 'The methods assume every layer has the same porosity and the same movable oil per unit thickness. What does a positive permeability-porosity correlation do to that?', '["It makes the true pore-volume-weighted coverage better than the thickness-weighted figure the method reports.", "It makes the reported coverage too high, since the fast layer holds less oil than its thickness implies.", "It changes the breakthrough ORDER, because a layer''s front velocity then depends on porosity as well as permeability.", "It has no effect on coverage, because porosity cancels between the numerator and denominator of the thickness weighting."]'::jsonb, 0, 'With permeability and porosity correlated, the layer that floods first also holds more than its share of the oil, so weighting by pore volume rather than thickness raises the true coverage above the reported one. The bias runs the other way from the option that reverses it. Front velocity in these methods is set by permeability and the shared pressure drop, so the order is unchanged, and porosity does not cancel out of a thickness-weighted sum.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-layered-sweep', 3, 'Piston displacement inside each layer is the third assumption. Which direction does it push the answer?', '["It understates the oil recovered per layer, because a real front leaves mobile oil that later production recovers.", "It overstates the time to breakthrough, because a real shock front travels faster than a piston front of the same volume.", "It overstates the oil recovered per layer at breakthrough, since the whole swept length is put at residual oil.", "It leaves recovery unchanged and affects only the producing water cut."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Piston displacement puts the whole layer behind the front at residual oil, when a real profile carries a saturation between the front value and residual, so the swept layer gives up less oil than the method credits it with. The producing water cut does also rise continuously rather than jumping, but that is a separate consequence, not a reason recovery is unaffected.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-layered-sweep', 4, 'The Dykstra-Parsons frontal position is x = (M - sqrt(M^2 + r(1 - M^2))) / (M - 1). At r = 0.5, what happens to x as M rises from 0.5 through 1.0 to 2.0?', '["It rises from 0.41886116991581024 through 0.5 to 0.5811388300841898, since a higher M drives the front harder.", "It falls from 0.5811388300841898 through 0.5 to 0.41886116991581024.", "It stays at 0.5 throughout, because the frontal position is fixed by the permeability ratio alone.", "It falls to a minimum at M = 1 and rises again, because the expression is indeterminate there and its limit is a turning point."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A favourable mobility ratio pushes the lagging front FURTHER along than proportionality alone would, and an unfavourable one holds it back, so x decreases as M increases. At M = 1 the expression is indeterminate and its limit is x = r, which is 0.5 here. That limit is a passing value on a monotone curve rather than a turning point, and holding x at r for all M is the Stiles assumption, not this one.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-layered-sweep', 5, 'In the Dykstra-Parsons water oil ratio, each layer still making oil is divided by x + M(1 - x). What is that factor?', '["A two-bank series resistance: the fluid crosses a water bank of length x and then an oil bank of length 1 - x, and series resistances add.", "A saturation correction converting the layer''s endpoint permeability to its value behind the advancing front.", "The fraction of the layer''s capacity that has already been swept, which reduces its remaining oil conductivity.", "A geometric factor accounting for the layer''s front being curved rather than planar over the distance x."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Along a partly-flooded layer the fluid crosses a water bank of length x and then an oil bank of length 1 - x, and resistances in series add, which is exactly where x + M(1 - x) comes from. It is a conductivity of the whole path rather than a saturation correction, a swept fraction or a geometric term. At the last stage every layer is through, no oil is left, and the water oil ratio is infinite.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-layered-sweep', 6, 'The Ekene column at M = 1.2 reaches a reservoir water oil ratio of 5 at the third breakthrough, where coverage is 0.82871309650611. What is the design reading of that?', '["The water oil ratio limit should be raised, because the remaining 32 feet of the 84 hold a sixth of the oil.", "The method has broken down at that stage, because a water oil ratio of 5 is outside the range Dykstra-Parsons is valid over.", "Coverage of 1 at the last stage is the number to design against, since continued injection does eventually reach every layer.", "About 0.83 rather than 1 is the vertical sweep this column can actually deliver within a plausible economic limit."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The last two layers contribute almost nothing in practice because reaching them costs a water oil ratio nobody will pay, so the economically reachable sweep is about 0.83 rather than 1. Coverage of 1 is achievable only in the sense that injection continues past a water oil ratio of 19 and then to infinity. The method has no validity ceiling in the water oil ratio, and a coverage number without a water oil ratio beside it is half a result.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-layered-sweep', 7, 'Stiles gives the surface water cut as A*Ci / (A*Ci + (Ct - Ci)), with A = M * Bo/Bw = 1.4304000000000001 for Ekene. What is A?', '["The ratio of swept to unswept capacity at the current stage, which rises from 0 to 1 through the flood.", "A fitting constant from the original Stiles correlation, calibrated on the field data the method was published with.", "The mobility ratio expressed in surface units, not an independent parameter.", "The endpoint mobility ratio corrected for the fraction of the column that has already broken through to the producer."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The first factor in A is the endpoint mobility ratio and the second, Bo/Bw, converts reservoir volumes to surface volumes. Nothing new enters. Ci/Ct is the swept capacity fraction and appears separately in the formula; A is neither fitted nor stage-dependent. The trailing digit on 1.4304000000000001 is binary arithmetic on a design value of 1.4304.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-layered-sweep', 8, 'In Stiles, A moves the surface water cut at first breakthrough from 0.4956992136055276 at A = 1 to 0.6628327528642564 at A = 2, while the coverage column does not move at all. Why not?', '["Coverage is thickness-weighted and A is capacity-weighted, so the two quantities use different weightings of the same layers.", "Stiles kinematics set the fronts by permeability alone, so the mobility ratio never enters its coverage column at all.", "A enters the coverage as well, but its effect cancels between the swept and unswept terms of the thickness sum.", "The coverage column is evaluated at the stage boundaries, where every front sits at either 0 or 1 regardless of A."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The distinguishing assumption of Stiles is that x = k_j/k_i, with no mobility dependence, so A can only appear where the method carries a mobility term, which is the water cut. That is precisely what separates it from Dykstra-Parsons. A more unfavourable ratio makes the broken-through layers carry a bigger share of the surface stream without making any front move differently.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-layered-sweep', 9, 'At M = 1 the Dykstra-Parsons and Stiles coverage columns are identical element by element, to the last bit. What produces exactness rather than closeness?', '["The frontal position''s limit at M = 1 is exactly x = r, the Stiles rule, and the engine implements that limit as a special case.", "Both methods reduce to the same thickness-weighted sum once the mobility terms are dropped from each of them.", "The engine computes the Stiles column first and reuses it whenever the mobility ratio is within tolerance of unity.", "At M = 1 the two-bank series resistance equals one, so the Dykstra-Parsons water oil ratio and coverage collapse together."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The frontal-position expression is indeterminate at M = 1 and its limit is exactly the Stiles kinematic rule, and the engine special-cases |M - 1| < 1e-9 so it literally computes that rule. Finding the limit where two related methods should coincide and confirming they do exactly, rather than to three decimals, is a good check on any such pair. The engine does not reuse one column for the other.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-layered-sweep', 10, 'Against Stiles, Dykstra-Parsons gives the lower coverage at every Ekene stage at M = 1.2, by 0.013663465827808197 at the first breakthrough. What happens at M below 1?', '["The gap widens in the same direction, because the mobility dependence grows as M moves away from 1 in either direction.", "The gap closes to zero and stays there, since Stiles is only defined for mobility ratios at or above unity.", "The sign reverses, and at a favourable mobility ratio Dykstra-Parsons predicts the better coverage of the two.", "Dykstra-Parsons stays lower but the gap becomes independent of M, because the frontal position saturates below unity."]'::jsonb, 2, 'At a favourable mobility ratio the mobility contrast helps the lagging layers catch up, so the method that carries the mobility term predicts BETTER coverage than the one that does not. The gap is signed by M - 1 rather than by its magnitude. Stiles is defined at any capacity ratio, and the frontal position does not saturate.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-layered-sweep', 11, 'Moving M from 0.5 to 5 on the Ekene column changes coverage at first breakthrough from 0.582878889525386 to 0.44357724095944917, and the water oil ratio from 0.7293534481099888 to 3.7041953452008216. What is the practical shape of that?', '["Both responses are roughly proportional to M, so a factor of ten in M is a factor of ten in the damage done.", "Coverage is the sensitive quantity and the water oil ratio is compressed, so conformance is where an intervention pays.", "The two responses are equal in relative terms, about a fifth in each, so either can stand for the other in a summary.", "An unfavourable flood reaches a similar fraction of the column and pays a great deal more water to get there."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A factor of ten in M moves coverage by under 14 points, a compressed response, while the water oil ratio moves by a factor of five, which is not compressed at all. The mobility ratio hurts mainly through the water it brings rather than the rock it fails to sweep. Confusing the two is easy because both get called sweep improvement, so name the quantity you claim to improve.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-layered-sweep', 12, 'For Ekene, M = 1.2 comes from endpoints 0.3 and 0.9 with viscosities 0.5 and 1.8 cp. Which of those four inputs is a practical design lever?', '["The oil viscosity, which can be lowered by raising the reservoir temperature through the injected water.", "The water viscosity, which is what a polymer flood raises.", "The two relative permeability endpoints, which a change of wettability through the injection brine will move.", "None of the four: M is fixed once the reservoir and its fluids are known, and only the well pattern can be changed."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The endpoints are rock properties and the oil viscosity is a reservoir property, which leaves the water viscosity as the one input a design routinely changes. Thermal and wettability methods do exist, but they are different processes rather than levers on a waterflood, and M is certainly not beyond reach. Raising the water viscosity fourfold takes M from 1.2 to 0.3.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-layered-sweep', 13, 'A polymer flood on Ekene would take M from 1.2 to about 0.3. What does the vertical sweep analysis say about that?', '["Coverage would improve by under ten points and the water oil ratio would roughly halve, leaving V untouched.", "Coverage would roughly double, since the mobility ratio and the coverage move together in near proportion.", "Neither coverage nor water oil ratio would move measurably, because both are set by the permeability distribution.", "Coverage would improve sharply and the water oil ratio would be unchanged, since A affects only the Stiles water cut."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Extrapolating the sensitivity gives an improvement under ten coverage points and roughly half the water oil ratio. Both are real gains and neither transforms the flood, because the column''s V = 0.5 heterogeneity is untouched by anything done to the fluid. Polymer helps most where the mobility ratio is badly unfavourable, which for a 1.8 cp oil it is not. This is a screening judgement and the course goes no further.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-layered-sweep', 14, 'The engine returns a warnings array with every layered analysis, naming piston displacement, no crossflow and equal porosity. What is the intended use of those strings?', '["They flag that the input layer set failed one of the engine''s validation checks and the result is provisional.", "They record which of the three assumptions the particular layer set actually violates, based on the data supplied.", "They travel with the number, and a report that quotes a coverage without them has stripped its caveats.", "They mark the result as unsuitable for anything beyond a first screening."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The strings are unconditional: they state the model''s standing assumptions and ride along with valid results. They are not conditional diagnostics of the input, not a validation failure, and not a grading of the run, since every run carries them. The point is that a coverage quoted without them reads as a claim rather than a screening estimate.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-layered-sweep', 15, 'At M = 1.2 the two methods differ by 1.4 coverage points at Ekene''s first breakthrough. What does that gap tell you about where to spend effort?', '["On choosing between the methods carefully, because a 1.4 point difference propagates into a large recovery difference.", "On the layer description, since at this M the choice of method changes little.", "On lowering the mobility ratio, because the gap between the methods measures how much a fluid intervention would return.", "On a simulator, because a disagreement between two screening methods means neither can be relied on for this column."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The gap between the two is an honest indication of what the kinematic assumption is worth, and at a mobility ratio this close to unity it is worth very little. Both methods carry the same three structural assumptions, which dominate the real uncertainty, so the layer description is where the leverage is. The gap measures method sensitivity rather than intervention value, and small disagreement is a reason for confidence rather than escalation.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-areal-sweep', 1, 'Why is the areal sweep at breakthrough less than 1 even when the mobility ratio is exactly 1?', '["Because the injected water is denser and slumps below the pattern, leaving the upper area untouched.", "Streamlines from injector to producer are short and fast on the direct line and long and slow round the sides, so water arrives before the corners are reached.", "Because part of the injected volume is lost to the aquifer at the pattern boundary before it can sweep.", "Because the producer draws from a radius smaller than the pattern, so the corners lie outside its drainage."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Areal sweep at breakthrough is a geometry result before it is a fluid one: even a perfectly stable front follows a streamline field in which the direct path is much the fastest, so water reaches the producer with the corners still unswept. The correlation gives 0.6838157924688618 at M = 1, so a third of the pattern is untouched with no mobility contrast at all. Density, aquifer loss and drainage radius are not what the correlation describes.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-areal-sweep', 2, 'What is the mechanism by which an unfavourable mobility ratio reduces the areal sweep at breakthrough?', '["Gravity override, in which the lighter oil is bypassed above the advancing water tongue.", "Capillary end effect at the producer, which holds water back until a threshold saturation is exceeded.", "Reduced injectivity at the injector, which lowers the volume delivered before the producer sees water.", "Viscous fingering: the more mobile water advances in narrow fingers rather than as a broad front."]'::jsonb, 3, 'At an unfavourable mobility ratio the displacing phase is more mobile than the displaced, which is unstable: perturbations on the front grow into fingers that run ahead along the fastest paths, so breakthrough happens having contacted much less area. This is the same M that set the layered frontal positions, doing a different job in a different dimension. Gravity override, capillary end effects and injectivity are separate mechanisms the correlation does not carry.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-areal-sweep', 3, 'The Craig five-spot correlation returns 0.6838157924688618 at M = 1 and is capped at 1 for M at or below about 0.15. What does the value at unity tell you?', '["That about a third of the pattern is unswept at breakthrough from geometry alone, which fluids then make worse.", "That the correlation is poorly constrained near unity, which is where its underlying experimental data set was sparsest.", "That a mobility ratio of 1 is the practical target, since sweep cannot improve past that point.", "That the remaining third is swept later, so the value at unity understates the true efficiency."]'::jsonb, 0, 'With no mobility contrast the front is as stable as it can be, and the correlation still leaves a third of the pattern uncontacted at breakthrough, which isolates the streamline geometry as the underlying cause. Sweep does improve below M = 1, reaching the cap of 1 near 0.15, which is the ideal a polymer flood chases. Later sweep is a separate correlation and does not change what happens at breakthrough.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-areal-sweep', 4, 'The engine raises a warning when the mobility ratio falls outside about 0.15 to 10. What is that range?', '["The range over which the underlying five-spot pattern remains hydraulically stable during injection.", "The range of mobility ratios encountered in the Ekene field''s own history-matched flood simulations.", "The correlation''s quoted validity range.", "The bounds the parameter validator enforces, beyond which the engine refuses to return a result at all."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Craig''s regression was published with a stated validity range and the engine warns rather than refuses outside it, so a result is still returned and is still the caller''s judgement to use. The range is a property of the source data, not of pattern stability, not of Ekene, and not a hard validation bound.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-areal-sweep', 5, 'Ekene has four producers and two injectors in whatever arrangement the drilling history left, and the forecast applies a five-spot correlation to it. How should that be reported?', '["As exact, since the correlation depends on the mobility ratio and not on the well arrangement.", "As an approximation with no error bar, since other pattern geometries carry different areal sweeps entirely.", "As conservative, because an irregular pattern always sweeps at least as well as the equivalent five-spot would.", "As invalid, so the areal sweep term should be set to 1 until a pattern-specific correlation is available."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A line drive, a nine-spot and an irregular arrangement each have their own areal sweep behaviour, and there is no general rule making an irregular pattern better or worse than a five-spot. So the substitution is an approximation whose error is not quantified, which is worth stating plainly rather than hiding. Setting the term to 1 would replace an approximation with something known to be wrong.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-areal-sweep', 6, 'How should a result built on the Craig correlation be quoted?', '["As a result quoted on the Craig five-spot correlation, naming explicitly the source that it all rests on.", "As a derived physical result, since the regression reproduces the streamline solution for a five-spot.", "Without qualification, because the correlation is standard across the industry and needs no attribution.", "As a simulated value, since the underlying data set was generated by numerical models rather than experiment."]'::jsonb, 0, 'It is a regression through 1950s experimental and simulated data, reproduced through several textbooks: a correlation rather than a derivation, carrying the character of its source. Using it is entirely standard and correct for screening, and naming it is what lets a reader judge it. The underlying data was partly experimental, so calling the output simulated is also wrong.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-areal-sweep', 7, 'The Dyes, Caudle and Erickson growth law is EA = min(1, EAbt + 0.2749 ln(Wi/WiBT)). What does its logarithmic form imply about the cost of extra sweep?', '["Every doubling of injected water past breakthrough buys the same 0.19054615993592894 of sweep, so the cost per unit of swept area doubles each time.", "Each doubling buys progressively more sweep, because the swept region grows faster as the front lengthens.", "Sweep grows without limit, so a long enough flood reaches every part of the pattern eventually.", "The first doubling buys most of the remaining sweep and later ones add almost nothing measurable."]'::jsonb, 0, '0.2749 times ln 2 is 0.19054615993592894 and it does not depend on where you start, so each doubling adds the same increment while the water needed to achieve it doubles. That is the shape that carries most of the economics of a mature waterflood. Growth is capped at 1 rather than unbounded, and the increments are equal rather than growing or collapsing.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-areal-sweep', 8, 'Starting from Ekene''s EAbt = 0.6573574366303985, at what injected volume ratio does the areal sweep reach 1?', '["At 2 times the breakthrough volume, where the correlation gives 0.8479035965663274 and the cap engages.", "At about 12.5 times, since the correlation must supply the whole remaining 0.34 of sweep logarithmically.", "It never reaches 1 exactly; the correlation approaches it asymptotically and the engine truncates at 0.99.", "At 3.477891445202633 times the breakthrough volume."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Solving EAbt + 0.2749 ln(x) = 1 gives x = exp((1 - 0.6573574366303985)/0.2749) = 3.477891445202633, a hand-checkable number. At twice the breakthrough volume the sweep is 0.8479035965663274 and the cap has not engaged. Three and a half breakthrough volumes is not a large multiple, which is why areal sweep is usually not the limiting efficiency in a mature flood.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-areal-sweep', 9, 'Given that areal sweep saturates at a little over three breakthrough volumes, which efficiencies usually limit a mature waterflood?', '["Areal sweep alone, since reaching the pattern corners consumes most of the injected water over the flood''s life.", "Vertical conformance and displacement efficiency, both still well below 1 at that point in the flood.", "Injectivity and voidage replacement, which set how quickly the breakthrough volume can be delivered at all.", "Displacement efficiency alone, since vertical conformance reaches 1 at the same time as the areal sweep does."]'::jsonb, 1, 'By three and a half breakthrough volumes the areal term has reached its ceiling while the Dykstra-Parsons coverage and the displacement efficiency are both still climbing slowly against a hard water oil ratio penalty. Vertical coverage reaches 1 only at the last layer''s breakthrough, long after the areal cap. Injectivity and voidage set the schedule rather than the ultimate efficiency.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-areal-sweep', 10, 'On the Ekene design case a water oil ratio limit of 10 and one of 25 stop the run at the same time with the same final water oil ratio of 31.119000015950355. What causes that?', '["The areal sweep has saturated at 1, so the oil rate collapses and the water oil ratio crosses both limits inside one monthly step.", "Both limits sit above the run''s horizon, so neither is ever reached and the run stops on elapsed time.", "The engine clamps any water oil ratio limit below 31 to the value at the last completed step before it.", "The two limits are within the correlation''s tolerance of each other, so the engine treats them as one case."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Once EA reaches its cap, further injection contacts no new rock, the oil rate falls away and the water oil ratio climbs very steeply. Both limits are crossed within a single monthly step, so the economic limit is resolved only to the step size and the reported final water oil ratio overshoots whichever limit stopped the run. Neither run stopped on the horizon and there is no clamping or tolerance behaviour involved.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-areal-sweep', 11, 'The Ekene displacement ceiling is EDmax = (1 - Swc - Sor)/(1 - Swc) = 0.4/0.65. What does that ratio express?', '["The fraction of the pattern pore volume that the injected water can physically occupy at residual oil.", "The maximum recovery factor of the pattern element, once the areal and vertical sweeps have both reached 1.", "The movable oil as a fraction of the oil initially present, in the rock the water actually passes through.", "The ratio of the water endpoint relative permeability to the oil endpoint, expressed as a saturation fraction."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The numerator 1 - Swc - Sor is the movable oil saturation and the denominator 1 - Swc is the oil saturation at the start, so the ratio is the largest fraction of the in-place oil that displacement alone can remove, giving 0.6153846153846154. It is a displacement quantity, not a pattern recovery factor: even at EA and EV of 1 the recovery factor of the element would still be bounded by it, and reaching it requires infinite throughput. Relative permeability endpoints are a different pair of numbers.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-areal-sweep', 12, 'The engine reports recoveryFactorOfFloodedOOIP = 0.5545614215589451 for the design case, while ED x EA x EV for the same case gives 0.2854276257199057. Which is wrong?', '["The engine value, which has omitted the vertical sweep term from the recovery factor it reports.", "Neither: they are fractions of different volumes, since EV is already inside the flooded volume.", "The triple product, which double counts the areal sweep already present in the displacement efficiency.", "The engine value, which uses the field-unit pore volume and so inherits the 7758 rounding error."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The engine computes its pattern volume as 7758 A h phi EV, with the vertical sweep already applied, so the flooded oil in place is the oil in the swept part of the column and its recovery factor must not carry EV a second time. Multiplying it in again applies the vertical sweep twice. The 7758 rounding is 46 parts per million and nowhere near a factor of two.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-areal-sweep', 13, 'A published waterflood recovery factor of 55 percent and one of 28 percent can describe the same flood. What must you establish before comparing two recovery factors?', '["The economic limit each run was stopped at, since a later stop always yields the larger recovery factor.", "The mobility ratio each was evaluated at, because areal and vertical sweep both depend on it strongly.", "Whether each was computed on a five-spot correlation or on a pattern-specific one with its own coefficients.", "The denominator each is a fraction of."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Recovery factor of the flooded pore volume is ED times EA, of the pattern element is ED times EA times EV, and of the field is smaller again. Three correct numbers, each a fraction of a different volume, and only the last belongs in a sentence beginning ''this flood will recover''. The stopping limit, the mobility ratio and the pattern correlation all matter to the value, but none of them makes 55 and 28 the same flood.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-areal-sweep', 14, 'At the end of the Ekene design case EA = 1, so dividing the reported recovery factor by EA should return the displacement efficiency alone. What does that check give and what does it establish?', '["0.5545614215589451 against a ceiling of 0.6153846153846154, so the run ended at about ninety percent of the maximum displacement efficiency.", "0.2854276257199057, which confirms the vertical sweep has been applied exactly once.", "0.6153846153846154 exactly, which would confirm the run reached the maximum displacement efficiency the Ekene endpoints allow before it stopped.", "1, which confirms that the areal sweep term has saturated as the correlation predicts."]'::jsonb, 0, 'With EA at exactly 1 the division returns the recovery factor unchanged, and comparing it against EDmax shows the run stopped at roughly ninety percent of the displacement ceiling, which is what a stop at a water oil ratio of 31 should look like. The same division mid-run, where EA sits between EAbt and 1, recovers the instantaneous displacement efficiency and is a useful check on any forecast output.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-areal-sweep', 15, 'The field-unit pore volume for the Ekene element is 11204911.22581217 rb against an exact metric 11205422.76570545 rb, a relative difference of -4.565110161166218e-5. Where does almost all of it come from?', '["The net thickness of 34.585155812896204 ft, which was rounded on conversion from the metric rock volume.", "The acre conversion of 4046.8564224 square metres, which is a rounded value rather than an exact definition.", "The constant 7758, which should really read 7758.354061806392 at the fixture''s own barrels per cubic metre.", "Repeated round trips between metric and field units in the chain, each adding its own small rounding."]'::jsonb, 2, 'An acre-foot is 4046.8564224 times 0.3048 cubic metres, both conversions exact by definition, which at 6.2898 barrels per cubic metre gives 7758.354061806392 barrels per acre-foot. The ratio 7758 over that is 0.9999543637988714, so the constant alone accounts for 99.97 percent of the observed gap and the remainder is rounding in the area and thickness chain. The acre and the foot are exact by definition, and the calculation converts once rather than repeatedly.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-the-forecast', 1, 'The forecast computes the water to breakthrough as WiBT = QiBt x PV x EAbt, giving 1253957.5213374475 rb for the design case. What is that expression saying?', '["That breakthrough occurs once the injected volume equals the pattern pore volume multiplied by the areal sweep, quite independent of the displacement solution.", "That the Welge construction supplies a volume in barrels which the areal sweep then reduces to the swept part.", "The 1-D breakthrough condition restated at pattern scale: the swept region has grown to EAbt of the pattern and carries the Welge profile.", "That the pore volumes injected at breakthrough are rescaled from the layer column to the pattern by the coverage."]'::jsonb, 2, 'QiBt is a pore-volume fraction from the Buckley-Leverett construction, PV is the flooded pattern volume and EAbt is the fraction of it contacted when water arrives, so the product is the 1-D result carried up to the pattern. QiBt = 0.33077027444818546 comes from the SCAL course as a fraction rather than a volume, the displacement solution is very much involved, and the coverage EV is already folded into PV rather than entering here.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-the-forecast', 2, 'The engine takes Np = max(Np, Np_previous) at every step. What is that guard for, and what does it cost?', '["It stops a small backward step where the pre- and post-breakthrough formulas meet, at the cost of hiding any genuine non-monotonicity in the output.", "It enforces material balance, which would otherwise drift as the rates are accumulated step by step.", "It prevents the displacement profile being interpolated outside the range the Welge construction covers.", "It compensates for the areal sweep cap, which would otherwise let recovery fall once EA reaches 1."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Both the areal sweep and the displacement efficiency are functions of Wi, and the transition between the two formulas near breakthrough can produce a small decrease in cumulative oil. Clamping is the cheap fix and the cost is that a real non-monotonicity would be invisible, which is the right trade for a screening tool as long as you know the clamp is there. Material balance closes by construction here, since the cumulative is computed rather than accumulated.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-the-forecast', 3, 'Refining the forecast time step from monthly to daily would change which reported quantities?', '["All three, since a smaller step reduces the accumulated numerical error in every marched quantity.", "The breakthrough day and the final water oil ratio, but not the cumulative oil at a fixed time.", "The cumulative oil only, since the rates are differenced from it and inherit whatever step is used.", "None of them: the correlations are closed form at any time."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The cumulative oil is computed from the volume and the efficiency at each time rather than accumulated, so it is exact at any step. Everything resolved by an event test is quantised: breakthrough is reported at the first step past WiBT and the run stops at the first step past the limit, so the final water oil ratio overshoots. The march does not integrate anything, so there is no accumulated error to reduce.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-the-forecast', 4, 'The forecast evaluates closed-form correlations and an interpolated displacement profile at a sequence of times. What follows about its numerics?', '["The step must satisfy a stability criterion based on the injection rate and the pattern pore volume.", "The solution converges as the step is refined, so a step-halving study is the way to establish accuracy.", "Numerical error accumulates across steps, so long runs at the thirty year horizon are the least reliable.", "There is no timestep stability or convergence behaviour to worry about."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Nothing is integrated, so none of the usual finite-difference concerns apply: there is no stability limit, no convergence to study and no accumulation of error. Refining the step improves the resolution of the reported EVENTS, which is a different thing from improving accuracy. Treating the march as an integrator is the misconception the lesson is built around.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-the-forecast', 5, 'Before breakthrough the forecast gives Np_rb = Wi exactly, so qo x Bo equals the injection rate. What kind of argument is that?', '["A displacement argument, resting on the shape of the fractional flow curve ahead of the front.", "A consequence of the areal sweep growing in proportion to injected volume before breakthrough.", "A volume argument: the reservoir is full and incompressible, and nothing but oil is leaving it yet.", "An enforced definition, since the engine computes the oil rate as the injection minus the water rate."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Push a barrel into a full incompressible system with one outlet and a barrel comes out; before breakthrough none of it is water. The identity holds whatever the relative permeability curves, the mobility ratio, the sweep or the geometry happen to be, which is exactly why it is a useful field check. The efficiencies decide when breakthrough happens and what follows, not whether the identity holds until then.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-the-forecast', 6, 'After breakthrough the design case still satisfies qo x Bo + qw x Bw = 2000 exactly on its last step. Can that be used to validate the forecast?', '["No: the engine defines the water rate as the injection minus the oil rate, so the balance is enforced rather than derived.", "Yes, since an exact volumetric balance holding at a water oil ratio above 31 is a strong independent check on the whole marched solution.", "Yes for the reservoir-barrel balance, but only once both rates have been converted back to surface volumes.", "No, because the balance holds only at the final step and cannot be checked at any earlier point in the run."]'::jsonb, 0, 'qw is computed as iw minus qo in reservoir barrels, so the sum reproduces the injection by construction at every step, not just the last one. A quantity computed by difference from an enforced identity inherits every error in the other terms and can never test them. The pre-breakthrough identity is a physical consequence; this post-breakthrough balance is a definition.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-the-forecast', 7, 'A field has injected for two years, no producer has made flood water, and the incremental oil is materially less than the injected reservoir volume. What does the pre-breakthrough identity tell you?', '["That the displacement efficiency is poor, so the relative permeability description needs revisiting.", "That one of three things is happening: injection is going out of zone, the reservoir is being repressurised rather than displaced, or the allocation is wrong.", "That breakthrough has already occurred somewhere unmonitored, since the identity holds only before it.", "That the areal sweep is worse than the correlation predicts for the pattern''s mobility ratio."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The identity is independent of every efficiency, so a shortfall cannot be blamed on displacement, sweep or geometry. It leaves exactly three explanations, and the Professional tier''s out-of-zone quantity and the Associate tier''s pressure track are the two measurements that size the first two. This is where the three tiers meet on one number.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-the-forecast', 8, 'The design case injects at 2000 rb/d and breaks through at 639.1875 days. At 1000 rb/d it breaks through at 1278.375 days with Np 1708898.2842386041, and at 4000 rb/d at 334.8125 days with Np 1711522.0925126772. What is the rule?', '["Rate buys oil, since the higher rate reaches a larger cumulative before the water oil ratio limit bites.", "Rate buys neither, because the breakthrough time scales with the injection rate and the two effects cancel exactly.", "Rate buys sweep, because a faster flood carries a more stable front and so contacts more of the pattern area.", "Rate buys time, not oil."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Quadrupling the rate moves breakthrough from 1278 days to 335, a factor of nearly four, and moves the ultimate cumulative oil by about 0.15 percent. The recovery is set by the efficiencies, which do not depend on the rate in these correlations, so the whole schedule compresses and the answer barely moves. Areal sweep here is a function of injected volume rather than of rate.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-the-forecast', 9, 'Raising the oil viscosity from 1.8 cp to 10 cp takes M from 1.2 to 6.666666666666667 and moves breakthrough from 639.1875 days to 365.25 days. Why does a worse mobility ratio break through EARLIER?', '["The more viscous oil resists displacement, so the injected water is forced along the direct streamline faster.", "The higher mobility ratio raises the injectivity, so more water is delivered in the same elapsed time.", "A smaller areal sweep at breakthrough gives a smaller WiBT, so less water is needed and it arrives sooner.", "The displacement efficiency falls, so less oil must be produced before the same injected volume breaks through."]'::jsonb, 2, 'WiBT = QiBt x PV x EAbt, and EAbt falls from 0.6573574366303985 to 0.5171816802422665 across that change, so less water is needed to reach the producer. The water arrives sooner and takes less oil with it, and the recovery factor falls from 0.5545614215589451 to 0.4743421828227725. The injection rate is an input here rather than a response, and the streamline geometry does not speed up.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-the-forecast', 10, 'Water oil ratio limits of 10 and 25 give identical results on the design case, both stopping at a final water oil ratio of 31.119000015950355. What does that reveal about the economic limit?', '["That it is resolved only to the step size, and the reported final water oil ratio overshoots whichever limit stopped the run.", "That the engine quietly applies the tighter of the two limits and then reports the value reached at the completed step before it.", "That a water oil ratio limit below about 31 has no effect, since the run is bounded by the horizon.", "That both limits fall inside the correlation''s tolerance, so the engine treats them as the same case."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The areal sweep saturates at 1, the oil rate collapses in that step and the water oil ratio crosses both limits within a single monthly interval, so both runs stop at the same step. The limit is an event test on a quantised march, so the value reported is the first one past the limit rather than the limit itself. Raising the limit to 50 does move the answer, to 2952.4375 days and a final water oil ratio of 52.33279393821437.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-the-forecast', 11, 'Ekene has no free gas, but running the design case with Sgi = 0.05 delays breakthrough from 639.1875 to 791.375 days while cumulative oil moves from 1709784.4164781766 to 1710241.7585443351 stb. What does fill-up do?', '["It reduces the ultimate recovery in proportion to the gas saturation that had to be refilled first.", "It delays the flood without diminishing it.", "It improves the ultimate recovery, because the compressed gas later expands and assists the displacement.", "It leaves both the timing and the recovery unchanged, since the gas volume is small next to the pore volume."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The fill-up volume PV x Sgi = 288353.19977680296 rb takes 144.17659988840148 days at 2000 rb/d, and the whole run shifts later by that time quantised to the step, which is 152.1875 days or exactly five monthly intervals. The ultimate oil is essentially unchanged. The engine has no gas expansion drive, and the delay is plainly not negligible.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-the-forecast', 12, 'At the fifth step of the fill-up run the engine reports a surface water cut of 0.7694267556885156 while the reservoir fractional flow at the outlet is 0. What is that volume actually?', '["Injected water short-circuiting to the producer through the part of the pattern already swept.", "Connate water mobilised by the pressure rise during fill-up, ahead of any displacement front.", "Free gas being pushed ahead of the injected water, which the engine carries no phase for at all.", "A numerical artefact of interpolating the Welge profile below the smallest tabulated pore volume injected."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Just past fill-up, Wi has only just become positive, so the cumulative oil and hence the oil rate are tiny while the injection is still 2000 rb/d, and the difference is booked as water because water is the only other phase the engine knows. The reservoir fractional flow of 0 in the same output says the displacement solution is producing no water at all. Sizing water treatment on that spike would be sizing for gas.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-the-forecast', 13, 'What is the signature that identifies the spurious water cut in a forecast output, and what should be done with those steps?', '["A high water cut in the first step or two after fill-up alongside a reservoir fractional flow of zero, and those steps are discarded for water handling design.", "A water cut above the final water oil ratio limit, and the run should be re-executed at a finer step.", "A negative cumulative oil increment, which the monotonicity guard has already clamped to zero.", "An areal sweep below its breakthrough value, and the whole pre-breakthrough period should be discarded."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Both numbers are in the output, and their disagreement is the tell: the enforced balance says water while the displacement solution says none. The right response is to drop those steps from any water handling calculation rather than to refine the step, which would not change the mechanism. The monotonicity clamp addresses a different problem near breakthrough.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-the-forecast', 14, 'The engine computes cumulative oil from the volume and the efficiency, then differences it to get rates, rather than accumulating rates. What does that buy?', '["Rates that are exact at every step, with the cumulative inheriting the interpolation error of the Welge profile.", "An exact cumulative at every single step, with the rates inheriting the resolution of the step size instead.", "Protection against the monotonicity problem, since a computed cumulative can never decrease between steps.", "Agreement with the ledger''s monthly volumes, since both are then built on the same mean-month interval."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Material balance closes by construction because the cumulative is evaluated rather than summed, and the price is that the rates are difference quotients whose resolution is the step. It does not protect against non-monotonicity, which is why the explicit max clamp exists. The ledger is a separate calculation on real volumes and this construction says nothing about agreeing with it.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-the-forecast', 15, 'displacementStateAtQi returns ED = 0.25443867265245035 with fw2 = 0 and beforeBT true at half of QiBt, and ED = 0.5088773453049006 with fw2 = 0.8682763300877854 at QiBt itself. What does the first pair describe?', '["A partially watered outlet, whose fractional flow is too small for the engine to report at that resolution.", "The state at the areal sweep''s breakthrough value, before the logarithmic growth law has taken over.", "An exhausted displacement profile, where the interpolation has fallen off the end of the Welge table.", "A dry outlet, with every injected barrel still displacing oil."]'::jsonb, 3, 'fw2 = 0 with beforeBT true says the outlet is producing no water at all, which is the regime in which the pre-breakthrough identity holds and every reservoir barrel injected returns as oil. At exactly half of QiBt the displacement efficiency is exactly half its breakthrough value, which is that identity restated. At 3 pore volumes the profile gives ED 0.5944963408073077 and fw2 0.9960946512691394, which is the far end of the same table.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-forecast-against-field', 1, 'The Ekene field injected 224975.42705121648 bbl over 1096 days into two elements, giving 104.68747061689818 rb/d per element against a design case of 2000. Is the field under-injecting?', '["Yes, by a factor of about twenty, which is the immediate cause of the poor recovery the forecast reports at that rate.", "No: the field has been meeting its voidage target throughout, and Ekene is simply a small field with a small voidage to replace.", "Yes, but only after 2025, when the injectivity loss at Ekene-4 reduced the volume that could be delivered per day.", "The question cannot be settled from the ledger, since voidage replacement and injection rate are unrelated quantities."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Four producers making a few hundred barrels of oil a day between them need about a hundred reservoir barrels a day per element to replace their voidage, and the ledger confirms the target was met, at a cumulative VRR of 1.034899536109. The 2000 rb/d design case is a pattern-scale illustration rather than a target the field failed. The injectivity step at Ekene-4 changed a pressure, not a volume.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-forecast-against-field', 2, 'Run at the observed 104.68747061689818 rb/d with EV = 1, the forecast reports no breakthrough in thirty years and stops on the horizon at 360 steps. How long would the breakthrough volume of 2436332.0258629364 rb take to deliver?', '["10957.5 days, which is the thirty year horizon itself, so breakthrough occurs exactly as the run ends.", "About 2436 days, since the breakthrough volume is quoted in reservoir barrels and the rate in barrels per day.", "It cannot be computed, because the areal sweep never leaves its pre-breakthrough proportional growth regime.", "23272.431853651782 days, or 63.71644586899872 years."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Dividing 2436332.0258629364 rb by 104.68747061689818 rb/d gives 23272.431853651782 days, so on this model the element would break through in the mid 2080s. The horizon of 10957.5 days is where the run stopped rather than where breakthrough sits, and both quantities are already in reservoir barrels. The pre-breakthrough regime is exactly where the arithmetic is simplest.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-forecast-against-field', 3, 'Ekene-6 made its first water 425 days after the flood started, while the forecast at the observed rate gives 63.72 years. Which adjustment closes that gap?', '["None of the model''s parameters: no plausible mobility ratio, vertical sweep, allocation or pattern correction moves a factor of fifty, so a structural assumption must be violated.", "Raising the mobility ratio to 10, which cuts the areal sweep at breakthrough and with it the breakthrough volume.", "Applying the vertical sweep of 0.5146907350993352, which halves the pore volume and so halves the time.", "Reallocating the whole field''s injection to a single element, which concentrates the delivered volume enough to match."]'::jsonb, 0, 'M = 10 takes 63.72 years to 48.29, the vertical sweep takes it to 32.79426436223196 years, and giving one element everything still leaves breakthrough many years away. A five-spot correlation on a non-five-spot arrangement is worth tens of percent, not factors of fifty. The two statements are not in tension over a parameter, they describe different processes, and the violated assumption is that the injected water sweeps the pattern element at all.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-forecast-against-field', 4, 'The channel back-out inverts WiBT = QiBt x PV x EAbt for PV. What changes role in that inversion?', '["QiBt stops being a Welge construction result and is fitted to the observed breakthrough date instead.", "EAbt stops being a correlation value and is replaced by the areal sweep implied by the allocation matrix.", "WiBT stops being a model output and becomes a measurement.", "PV stops being a booked volume and is taken from the layer column''s thickness-weighted coverage instead."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The water actually delivered to Ekene-6 by the day it made water is an observation from the injection record and the allocation, so the equation can be solved for the one remaining unknown. QiBt = 0.33077027444818546 and EAbt = 0.6573574366303985 stay exactly what they were, from the SCAL Welge construction and the Craig correlation. PV is the answer here rather than an input.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-forecast-against-field', 5, 'The back-out gives an implied contacted pore volume of 164686.15596920124 rb against an element of 11205422.76570545 rb. Why is 1.4697005138728763 percent described as an upper bound?', '["Because the allocation matrix understates the volume Ekene-6 actually received, so the true numerator of the inversion is larger than the one that was used.", "Every assumption in the inversion is generous to the sweep, so a poorer displacement profile implies a smaller contacted volume still.", "Because water continued to arrive after the breakthrough date, so the eventual contacted volume must exceed it.", "Because the measurement is a date rather than a volume, and dates are quantised to the monthly ledger period."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The inversion credits the arriving water with a full Buckley-Leverett profile behind its front at the areal sweep the correlation gives, which is the most favourable reading available. If it channelled through a narrow path with a poorer profile, the volume it contacted is smaller. Later arrivals are a different question: the bound is on the water that arrived FIRST, which is what makes it a statement about a channel.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-forecast-against-field', 6, 'Suppose Ekene-6 actually received ALL of the field''s injection before its breakthrough, 85625.10520691355 bbl rather than the allocated 35106.29313483455. What happens to the conclusion?', '["The implied swept fraction rises to 3.584635399689942 percent, still under a twenty-eighth of the element.", "The implied swept fraction rises above a quarter of the element, which is within the range of normal sweep.", "The conclusion inverts, because the numerator scales by 2.439024390243903 and the denominator is unchanged.", "The inversion becomes undefined, since the allocation matrix is the only source of the numerator volume."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A factor of 2.439024390243903 on the numerator moves a percent-level answer to another percent-level answer. To reach even a quarter of the element, Ekene-6 would have needed 17.010268258069352 times its allocated share, which is seven times more water than the whole field injected. The result is robust to the allocation by a wide margin, which is what you want from a conclusion that rests on a judgement.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-forecast-against-field', 7, 'What kind of evidence is the channel back-out?', '["A shape argument, since it depends on the form of the fractional flow curve behind the advancing front.", "A statistical argument, since the allocation shares are fitted to the observed breakthrough times of all four producers.", "A volumetric argument built from a date, a volume, two dimensionless numbers and one booked pore volume from geoscience.", "A material balance argument, since it closes the injected and produced volumes over the pre-breakthrough period."]'::jsonb, 2, 'It uses a date from the production record, a volume from the injection record and the allocation, two dimensionless numbers from published correlations and the displacement construction, and a pore volume from the geoscience booking. It uses no curve shapes, no classification thresholds, no fitted parameters and no judgement beyond the allocation matrix, which is precisely what makes it argue with the Chan diagnostic rather than restate it.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-forecast-against-field', 8, 'Water reaching a producer through 1.5 percent of the intervening pore volume says what about the two jobs a flood does?', '["Neither job is being done, since water confined to a channel neither displaces oil nor supports the pressure.", "Both jobs are being done, in a small volume that widens over time.", "The displacement job is being done in the channel and the pressure job is failing, which the survey cadence hides.", "The pressure job is being done and measured, while the displacement job is not being done at all."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The voidage ledger confirms the field replaced its voidage and the pressure recovered 34.4931292839633 psi from its trough, so the pressure job is real and measured. Water that contacts one sixty-eighth of the rock displaces oil from one sixty-eighth of the rock, so the displacement job is not being done. That is the two-jobs distinction from the first Associate lesson with a number now attached to each half.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-forecast-against-field', 9, 'Given the channelling result, what is the correct response to the north element''s over-injection?', '["Move water from north to south immediately, since the pattern cumulative VRRs of 1.20 and 0.61 already quantify the imbalance precisely enough to act on.", "Conformance control on the northern path before any redistribution, since raising or moving rate sends more water down the same route.", "Increase total injection, since the element is evidently losing water and needs more to reach the unswept rock.", "Do nothing until a tracer confirms the path, since no action is justified on two indirect indications."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The Professional tier''s redistribution is the right arithmetic on the wrong premise if most of the northern water is short-circuiting: it would move water from a path that is not sweeping to a pattern whose own sweep is unmeasured. Increasing rate sends more water down the same channel. Waiting for a tracer is defensible on the measurement but the conclusion required all three tiers and is strong enough to act on the cheap intervention first.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-forecast-against-field', 10, 'The Chan diagnostic and the volumetric back-out reach the same conclusion about Ekene. What do the two arguments share?', '["Essentially nothing except the field: one uses producer rates, derivatives and a log-log slope, the other uses volumes, a date and two correlations.", "The allocation matrix, which supplies the per-producer water rates to Chan and the numerator to the back-out.", "The Welge construction, which supplies the fractional flow to Chan and the pore volumes injected to the back-out.", "The five-spot correlation, which sets the pattern geometry both methods assume when attributing water to a producer."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Chan takes producer oil and water rates and applies numerical differentiation and a log-log regression, with no volumes, no allocation and no pattern geometry. The back-out takes an injection record, an allocation matrix, a booked pore volume and two correlations, with no curve shapes, no derivatives and no thresholds. Two hypotheses failing in the same direction from disjoint inputs is a much stronger position than either alone.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-forecast-against-field', 11, 'How far does the agreement between the two diagnostics actually go?', '["It establishes channelling, since two independent methods agreeing is the standard of proof in reservoir engineering.", "It establishes channelling at Ekene-6 and Ekene-3, since Chan classifies both producers above the 0.4 threshold.", "Not far: both results are dominated by Ekene-6, only one of the two is quantitative, and neither identifies the path.", "It establishes nothing, since both methods were built on the same designed water histories and share that origin."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The field-level Chan aggregate is essentially a restatement of Ekene-6 and the back-out was computed for Ekene-6, so two methods agree about one well rather than across four. The back-out says 1.47 percent and Chan says channelling-like with a hedge. Neither distinguishes a thin high-permeability streak from a fracture, a fault or communication behind casing, and the treatment differs for each.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-forecast-against-field', 12, 'What would the two agreeing diagnostics actually establish?', '["That the channelling hypothesis is confirmed, so the conformance treatment can be designed without further data.", "That a direct measurement is now worth paying for, which is a decision to be taken rather than a conclusion.", "That the allocation matrix is validated by the agreement.", "That the Chan thresholds are calibrated for this field, since the volumetric result independently confirms them."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Agreement between two indirect methods, one of them qualitative, raises confidence without proving anything. What it changes is the economics of finding out: a tracer costs real money and months of waiting, and two independent indications justify that spend. Chan does not rest on the allocation at all, so nothing here validates it.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-forecast-against-field', 13, 'Which test would falsify the channelling hypothesis most cheaply, and should be done first?', '["A tracer injected at Ekene-2, whose transit time gives the path length and whose return fraction gives connectivity.", "A pressure interference test between Ekene-2 and Ekene-6, which would show whether the two are well connected.", "A repeat Dykstra-Parsons fit on core plug permeabilities, to test whether the layer model understates the heterogeneity.", "A chemical analysis of the water at Ekene-6."]'::jsonb, 3, 'If the water at Ekene-6 is formation water rather than injected water then it never came from the flood and both diagnostics are reading a coincidence, and that is settled by analysing a sample. The tracer is the measurement that would settle the path question but costs money and months. A poor interference result would break the allocation and with it the back-out''s numerator, which is also informative but not the cheapest first move.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-forecast-against-field', 14, 'The design case leaves about seventy percent of the element''s oil behind. Where does the Ekene evidence say most of it is?', '["In rock the water never went near, which points at conformance rather than at the chemistry of residual oil in swept rock.", "As residual oil behind the displacement front, which needs a surfactant or a miscible solvent to recover.", "Distributed evenly between the three places, so the screening should weight all three technologies equally.", "In the unswept layers specifically, which polymer would address by improving the mobility ratio."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Residual oil in swept rock, oil in unswept pattern area and oil in unswept layers are three targets needing three different technologies, and the first step in screening is deciding which holds the oil. If water reaches Ekene-6 through 1.47 percent of the pore volume, the dominant loss is rock the water never contacted. Conformance treatments are also generally much cheaper than chemical EOR, so the evidence points at the cheaper intervention first.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-forecast-against-field', 15, 'Why does this course stop at qualitative EOR screening rather than producing a number?', '["Because EOR decisions belong after the waterflood has finished, which for Ekene is decades away yet.", "Because the Craig and Dyes-Caudle-Erickson correlations are not valid outside the mobility ratio range a polymer flood would reach.", "There is no validated central EOR screening engine, and a course is built on validated math rather than before it.", "Because the displacement construction imported from the SCAL course already prices polymer, so a second treatment would duplicate it."]'::jsonb, 2, 'New engine math lands with goldens and validation first and a course follows, and reversing that order produces courses teaching numbers nobody has checked. So nothing in the module is graded and no EOR figure appears in the capstone. Most EOR decisions are in fact made while the flood is still running, since the flood''s own performance is the main evidence about where the remaining oil is.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 1, 'What conclusion about Ekene required all three tiers together, and is contained in none of them alone?', '["That the field should raise total injection, since the Associate ledger shows voidage replacement only just above unity.", "That the correct response to the north-south imbalance is conformance control on the northern path, before any redistribution of water.", "That the pressure survey cadence should be tightened considerably, since the six-monthly surveys missed the 2023-04 pressure trough entirely and read it three months late.", "That the allocation matrix should be refitted, since the out-of-zone fraction of 12 percent is too large to leave as judgement."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The Associate tier''s pressure recovery is real, the Professional tier''s imbalance between 1.20 and 0.61 is real, and this tier says most of the water in the over-injected element is short-circuiting, so moving water from north to south would redistribute water that is not sweeping. The cadence and the allocation are both genuine findings of their own tiers, and neither of them needed the other two.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 2, 'Capstone field 1 asks for the permeability variation V. What two conventions decide whether your answer matches?', '["The plotting position (i + 0.5)/n on a zero-based DESCENDING sort, giving 0.1, 0.3, 0.5, 0.7 and 0.9, and V = 1 - exp(-sigma) with sigma the negative fitted slope.", "The ascending sort order and the choice of natural rather than base-ten logarithms in the regression.", "The krFloor applied to the permeabilities and the minimum of three positive values the engine requires.", "The thickness weighting applied to each permeability and the net pay total the weights are normalised by."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A different plotting position convention gives something untidy in the second decimal, so a clean round answer is itself the sign that the convention is right. The sort is descending and the slope of ln k against the normal quantile is negative, so sigma is its negation. Thickness never enters V at all, and the floor and the minimum count are unrelated to the convention.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 3, 'Capstone field 2 asks for the Dykstra-Parsons coverage at first breakthrough. What is the likeliest way to get a plausible wrong answer?', '["Evaluating the frontal position at the Stiles capacity ratio of 1.4304000000000001 rather than at the mobility ratio of 1.2 that the method takes.", "Taking the coverage at the third breakthrough rather than the first, since the stage table is indexed from zero.", "Pairing the depth-ordered thicknesses with the permeability-ordered layers, so the fastest layer is taken as 18 feet rather than 22.", "Weighting the frontal positions by permeability-thickness rather than by thickness alone, as the water oil ratio does."]'::jsonb, 2, 'normalizeLayers sorts by permeability descending and shuffles the thickness column with it, from 18, 22, 16, 14, 14 to 22, 14, 16, 18, 14. Sorting the columns separately rather than the pairs gives a coverage that looks entirely reasonable and is wrong. The capacity ratio belongs to Stiles, and the kh weighting belongs to the water oil ratio rather than to the coverage.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 4, 'What check confirms your capstone field 2 answer is in the right place?', '["It exceeds the Stiles coverage at the same stage, because Dykstra-Parsons carries the extra mobility dependence.", "It matches the permeability variation V of 0.5, since the coverage at first breakthrough tracks the heterogeneity.", "It equals the areal sweep at breakthrough of 0.6573574366303985, since both describe the swept fraction at the same moment.", "It is a little above half, and slightly below the Stiles coverage at the same stage, because the mobility ratio exceeds 1."]'::jsonb, 3, 'At M above 1 Dykstra-Parsons is the more pessimistic of the two, so 0.5146907350993352 sits just under the Stiles 0.5283542009271434. The closeness of the coverage to V here is a coincidence of this column at this mobility ratio and not a relationship to lean on. The areal sweep is a fraction of the pattern AREA and is a different quantity entirely.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 5, 'Capstone field 3 asks for the Stiles surface water cut at first breakthrough. A learner who uses M in place of A returns 0.4956992136055276. What have they omitted?', '["The Bo/Bw factor that converts the reservoir mobility ratio into surface units, which is the whole difference between M and A.", "The capacity weighting kh on each of the layers, which the surface water cut expression applies to the broken-through layers only.", "The thickness normalisation, which converts the capacity ratio from a fraction of the column to a fraction of unity.", "The frontal position of the unbroken layers, which enters the surface water cut through the series resistance."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A = M x Bo/Bw = 1.2 x 1.21584/1.02 = 1.4304000000000001, so dropping the formation volume factors leaves A = M = 1.2 and gives 0.4956992136055276 rather than the graded 0.5843728303284756. That is close enough to look right, which is what makes it the dangerous mistake. The capacity weighting and the thickness sums are present in both versions, and Stiles carries no series resistance.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 6, 'Capstone field 4 asks for the areal sweep at breakthrough from the Craig correlation. What input does it take?', '["The capacity ratio 1.4304000000000001, since the correlation was published in surface volumes throughout.", "The permeability variation 0.5, since areal sweep and vertical sweep are both driven by the heterogeneity.", "The mobility ratio 1.2, not the Stiles capacity ratio 1.4304000000000001.", "The pore volumes injected at breakthrough 0.33077027444818546, since the correlation is parameterised on throughput."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Craig''s regression is a function of the mobility ratio alone, and evaluating it at 1.4304000000000001 returns a lower areal sweep that looks entirely plausible. The capacity ratio exists only inside Stiles. Neither the permeability variation nor the pore volumes injected appear in the correlation, which has four explicit terms in M.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 7, 'Capstone field 5 computes the element pore volume as 7758 A h phi EV. What happens if EV is left out?', '["The answer is unchanged, because the vertical sweep cancels between the pore volume and the breakthrough volume.", "The answer roughly halves, because a larger pore volume is reached by the same injected water sooner.", "The engine rejects the case, because a pore volume without a vertical sweep multiplier fails its validation.", "The answer roughly doubles."]'::jsonb, 3, 'EV = 0.5146907350993352 nearly halves the volume, so omitting it nearly doubles both PV and the breakthrough volume WiBT = QiBt x PV x EAbt, and hence the time to deliver it at a fixed rate. It does not cancel, since WiBT is proportional to PV and the injection rate is an independent input. A larger volume takes longer to fill, not less time, and the engine validates no such thing.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 8, 'Computing capstone field 5 without the step quantisation gives 626.9787606687237 days against a graded 639.1875. Why does the engine report the larger value?', '["It adds a half-step correction to centre the reported breakthrough within the interval it occurred in.", "It applies the monotonicity clamp, which cannot report a breakthrough earlier than the previous step''s value.", "It uses a mean month of 30.4375 days for the march and a calendar month for the reported date, and the two differ.", "It reports the first whole monthly step at which the cumulative injected volume exceeds WiBT."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Breakthrough is an event test on a quantised march, so it fires at the first step past the threshold rather than at the exact crossing, which puts it one step of 30.4375 days late. The 12 day difference is inside the tolerance''s reach, so dropping the quantisation is a detectable error. There is no centring correction, the clamp addresses cumulative oil, and only one month length is in use.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 9, 'Capstone field 6 divides the implied contacted volume by a pore volume. Which one, and why?', '["The whole element at 11205422.76570545 rb, computed WITHOUT the vertical sweep multiplier, because the question is what fraction of the element the water contacted.", "The flooded volume of 5767063.995536059 rb, since that is the volume the displacement construction applies to.", "The field-unit volume of 11204911.22581217 rb, since the forecast engine works throughout in field units.", "The volume of the layer that broke through first, since that is where a channelling path would necessarily sit."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Using the vertically-reduced flooded volume doubles the answer, which is the likeliest mistake in the field and is worth stating twice. The field-unit and metric element volumes differ by 46 parts per million, far inside the tolerance, so either is acceptable; the vertical sweep multiplier is not. A channel need not follow the fastest layer of the model column.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 10, 'What magnitude check tells you capstone field 6 is right?', '["It is a tens-of-percent number, consistent with the vertical coverage at first breakthrough of about 0.51.", "It matches the out-of-zone fraction of 12 percent, since both measure water that failed to sweep its target.", "It is a percent-level number, and a tens-of-percent answer means the denominator used was the wrong volume.", "It exceeds the areal sweep at breakthrough of 0.6573574366303985, since the contacted volume includes it."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The graded answer is 1.4697005138728763 percent, about one sixty-eighth of the element, and the only realistic way to land in the tens of percent is to have divided by the vertically-reduced volume or by something smaller still. The out-of-zone fraction is a different quantity from a different tier, and the areal sweep is a fraction of area rather than of contacted pore volume.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 11, 'The lesson says every field in this capstone depends on getting one ordering, one convention or one denominator right. What does it recommend before submitting?', '["Recomputing each field twice at different time steps, so that any quantisation artefact shows up as a difference.", "Writing down, for each answer, which volume or which ordering it is relative to.", "Checking each field against the panel tiles, since all six are readable there at the default settings.", "Reporting each field to the full precision the engine returns, so that a near-miss can be distinguished from a wrong method."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The arithmetic is not the hard part in this tier, and half the near-misses come from that sentence not being written. Only fields 1 to 5 are readable in the panel and field 6 has to be worked from the definition, so the panel cannot serve as the universal check. Precision and step refinement address different failure modes than the ones this capstone actually produces.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 12, 'Cumulative, rolling and instantaneous VRR; field and pattern VRR; recovery factor of the flooded volume and of the element; reservoir fractional flow and surface water cut. What do these pairs have in common?', '["In each pair one number is a reservoir quantity and the other its surface equivalent, related by a formation volume factor.", "In each pair the second is a refinement of the first, so the later number supersedes the earlier one in a report.", "In each pair the difference is small enough that either can be quoted provided the convention is stated alongside.", "In each pair both of the numbers are correct, and only one of the two answers the question actually in front of you."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Every serious error in the course came from that confusion rather than from arithmetic, which is why naming the question a number answers is the first of the three things the course set out to teach. Only one of the four pairs is a reservoir-to-surface conversion, none is a refinement of the other, and the recovery factors differ by a factor of two, which is not small.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 13, 'The pressure a formation volume factor was evaluated at, whether the solution gas subtraction fired, whether a Hall plot used absolute or above-reference pressure, which plotting position a fit used. What do these share?', '["None of them is discoverable from the answer, so whether they travel with a number is the difference between a reproducible result and an anecdote.", "Each is a validation check the engine performs and reports in its warnings array before returning a result.", "Each moves the answer by less than a percent, so they matter for reproducibility rather than for a decision.", "Each is set once for a field and then held constant, so they need stating only when a new field is set up."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A reader handed 1.034899536109 cannot tell which formation volume factor set produced it, and a reader handed a Hall slope ratio cannot tell which pressure convention it used, and in the Hall case the two conventions differ between raising one alert and raising none. Some of these move the answer by a great deal, and a convention chosen per analysis rather than per field is exactly why it has to travel with the number.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 14, 'The withheld pattern advice that gives a reason instead of an assumed split, the producers excluded from the Chan table for insufficient data, the forecast''s warnings about piston layers. What habit do these illustrate?', '["A tool should degrade gracefully, substituting a documented default whenever an input is missing or too sparse.", "A tool that says plainly what it does not know is far more useful than one that always returns a number regardless.", "A tool should refuse to return any result at all once one of its stated assumptions cannot be verified from the data.", "A tool should report its uncertainty numerically, so that a caller can propagate it through to the final answer."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The engine does not invent a split, does not classify a producer on six wet months against a ten-point minimum, and states its structural assumptions unconditionally, and the habit to copy is to make your own work behave the same way. None of the three is a graceful default, an outright refusal or a numerical uncertainty: each is a refusal to answer a specific question the data cannot settle, with the reason attached.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 15, 'The course''s closing advice includes building a forecast in order to be surprised by it. What does that mean in practice?', '["A forecast should be run at several parameter settings so that the spread of outcomes represents the uncertainty honestly.", "A forecast should be built before the ledger, so that the data gathering is directed by what the model turns out to need.", "A forecast that agrees with the field teaches nothing, while one that disagrees structurally names the violated assumption.", "A forecast should be tuned until it matches the history, so that its extrapolation forward carries the field''s own behaviour."]'::jsonb, 2, 'At the observed injection rate the Ekene forecast is wrong by a factor of fifty, and because its assumptions are stated the disagreement identifies which one is violated, which produced the most valuable number in the tier. Tuning until it matched would have hidden exactly that. The ledger comes first in the recommended order, since everything above it inherits its errors.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'final', null, 1, 'Two columns hold the same five permeabilities but assign them to different depths. What differs between them?', '["Their permeability variation V, since the fit runs down the column in the order the layers are supplied.", "Their stage coverages, since the thickness attached to each permeability changes with the assignment.", "Nothing that the Dykstra-Parsons or Stiles analysis computes, but a great deal about gravity and completion design.", "Neither V nor the coverages, and nothing else either, since the sweep methods are complete descriptions."]'::jsonb, 2, 'V and the stage tables both depend only on the sorted permeability-thickness pairs, so reassigning the same permeabilities to different depths leaves both untouched. A 600 md streak at the base and the same streak at the top give identical predictions here and very different floods once gravity segregation or a selective completion enters, neither of which these methods model.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'final', null, 2, 'What does the engine''s inverse normal return for a probability of 0 or 1?', '["NaN, since the function is defined only on the open interval.", "Zero, which the caller must then test for as a sentinel value.", "Plus or minus a large finite bound, chosen so that the regression stays conditioned.", "The value at the nearest representable interior probability, which is a silent clamp."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The standard normal quantile is unbounded at both ends, so there is no finite answer to return and the approximation is defined on the open interval only. The engine''s plotting position (i + 0.5)/n never produces 0 or 1, which is one of the reasons that convention was chosen. The function is antisymmetric about 0.5, giving minus and plus 1.2815515641401563 at 0.1 and 0.9.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'final', null, 3, 'The engine''s own sample column of 500, 250, 120, 60 and 30 md returns V = 0.6787019773471668 against Ekene''s 0.5. What does that say?', '["It is the better characterised of the two, since a higher V means the log-normal fit is tighter.", "It is the more homogeneous of the two, since V approaching 1 describes a uniform permeability set.", "The two cannot be compared, since the sample column has a different median permeability of 121.97554094669343.", "It is the more heterogeneous of the two columns, so its flood would lose more of its oil to conformance."]'::jsonb, 3, 'V rises toward 1 with increasing spread, so 0.68 is a more heterogeneous column than 0.5 and sits nearer the 0.8 threshold above which a conventional waterflood has severe conformance problems. V says nothing about goodness of fit, which is judged on whether the points fall on a line. Comparing V across columns is exactly what it is for, and the median is a separate parameter.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'final', null, 4, 'In the Dykstra-Parsons fit, what is regressed against what, and what does each fitted parameter become?', '["k against the plotting position directly; the slope is V and the intercept is the median permeability.", "ln k against the normal quantile of the plotting position; the slope is minus sigma and the intercept is ln k50.", "ln k against the cumulative thickness fraction; the slope is sigma and the intercept is the arithmetic mean permeability.", "The normal quantile against ln k; the slope is 1 over sigma and the intercept is the quantile of the median."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Sorting descending makes ln k decrease as the probability increases, so the fitted slope is negative and sigma is its negation, while exponentiating the intercept gives k50. Thickness enters nowhere in this fit. Regressing the other way round would give a different least squares line, since least squares is not symmetric in its two variables.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'final', null, 5, 'Typical reservoir sands run V between about 0.5 and 0.8. What is the significance of the upper end?', '["Above about 0.8 a conventional waterflood will have severe vertical conformance problems throughout.", "Above about 0.8 the log-normal assumption fails and the fitted V stops being interpretable at all.", "Above about 0.8 the Dykstra-Parsons frontal position formula leaves its published validity range.", "Above about 0.8 the engine raises a warning and falls back to the Stiles kinematic treatment instead."]'::jsonb, 0, 'V above 0.8 is a screening gate: it says a conventional flood will lose a great deal to vertical conformance and that conformance control belongs in the plan from the start. It is a statement about the flood rather than about the fit or the method, both of which remain perfectly well defined, and the engine has no such fallback.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'final', null, 6, 'What does normalizeLayers do with a layer whose thickness or permeability is zero or negative?', '["Retains it with a front position of zero, so it contributes thickness without ever breaking through.", "Raises a validation error, since a column containing a non-positive entry cannot be analysed.", "Drops it before sorting, so it appears in no stage table.", "Clamps it to the smallest positive value in the set, so the sort order is preserved."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Rows with non-positive thickness or permeability are removed and the survivors are sorted by permeability descending, so the analysis proceeds on a shorter list. Nothing is clamped and nothing errors, which is convenient and is also a reason output indices do not refer back to input indices. The minimum of three positive permeabilities applies to the V fit rather than to this normalisation.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'final', null, 7, 'Why is k50 from the fit the median permeability rather than the mean?', '["Because the median of a set of five values is the third one, which the regression passes through exactly.", "Because the intercept sits at a quantile of exactly zero, which is the median of the fitted distribution.", "Because the least squares fit minimises absolute rather than squared deviations, which returns a median.", "Because the arithmetic mean of a log-normal is undefined, so the median is the only available centre."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The regression is of ln k against the normal quantile, and the quantile of 0.5 is zero, so the intercept is ln of the value at the median probability. For a log-normal the mean sits above the median and is perfectly well defined; it is simply not what an intercept at zero quantile returns. The fit is ordinary least squares on the whole set rather than a pass through the middle point.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'final', null, 8, 'Why is the Dykstra-Parsons water oil ratio infinite at the last stage?', '["The frontal position of the last layer reaches exactly 1, which puts a zero in the series resistance term.", "The coverage reaches 1, and the water oil ratio is defined as the coverage divided by one minus itself.", "The capacity of the broken layers reaches the total capacity, so the numerator grows without bound.", "Every layer has broken through, so no layer is still making oil."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The denominator sums over layers with index above the stage, and at the last stage that sum is empty, so the ratio has no oil to divide by. The series resistance x + M(1 - x) evaluates to 1 rather than 0 at x = 1, the coverage and the water oil ratio are separate quantities, and the numerator is bounded by the total capacity.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'final', null, 9, 'The Ekene column reaches a reservoir water oil ratio of 19.26213317694089 at the fourth breakthrough, with a fifth of the column still unreached. What is the shape that describes?', '["Coverage improving slowly while the water oil ratio deteriorates fast, which is the signature of vertical conformance loss.", "Coverage and water oil ratio deteriorating together, so either can be used as the conformance indicator.", "A flood approaching its displacement ceiling, where further injection recovers residual oil only.", "An areal sweep problem, since a water oil ratio that high implies the water is bypassing the pattern corners."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Between the first and fourth breakthroughs the coverage moves from 0.5146907350993352 to 0.9282289679872078 while the water oil ratio goes from 1.099451244867303 to over 19. The last increments of coverage cost a water oil ratio nobody will pay, which is why a coverage number without a water oil ratio beside it is half a result. This is a vertical calculation with no areal or displacement content in it.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'final', null, 10, 'Stiles predates Dykstra-Parsons by a year and makes one simplifying move. What does it give in exchange?', '["A frontal position valid at any mobility ratio, since dropping the mobility term removes the restriction.", "The producing water cut directly in surface units, which is exactly what an operator actually measures.", "A coverage that already includes the areal sweep, so no separate pattern correlation is needed.", "An explicit economic limit, since the water cut can be compared against a facility constraint directly."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Stiles assumes fronts advance in proportion to permeability alone and returns the surface water cut, which is what a test separator reports and what a facility is sized for. Dropping the mobility term restricts rather than extends its validity, to mobility ratios near unity. Areal sweep is a separate calculation, and comparing against a limit is the user''s job in either method.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'final', null, 11, 'Converting a Dykstra-Parsons water oil ratio to a Stiles-style water cut needs more than fw = WOR/(1 + WOR). What else?', '["The coverage at that stage, because the water cut is weighted by the swept thickness fraction.", "The capacity ratio A in place of the mobility ratio M, because the two methods index their stages differently.", "The Bo/Bw factor, because the one is at reservoir conditions and the other at surface conditions.", "The connate water saturation, because the surface water stream includes produced connate water as well."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The Dykstra-Parsons water oil ratio is a reservoir-condition ratio and the Stiles water cut is a surface fraction, so the conversion carries the formation volume factors, which is precisely the factor sitting inside A. A water oil ratio of 1.099451244867303 corresponds to a reservoir water cut of about 0.524, while the Stiles surface figure at the same stage is 0.5843728303284756.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'final', null, 12, 'What does the no-crossflow assumption actually require of the reservoir?', '["A mobility ratio near unity, so that pressure differences between adjacent layers stay small enough to ignore.", "Equal porosity in every layer, so that the pressure at a given distance is the same in each of them.", "A horizontal reservoir, so that gravity does not drive fluid across the layer boundaries between beds.", "Continuous impermeable barriers between every pair of layers, over the whole distance between the wells."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Isolation over the whole path is a geological requirement, and real shale breaks are laterally discontinuous, so where a barrier is absent the fast layer crossflows into its neighbour. That is a structural omission rather than an approximation: when crossflow is significant the model answers a different question. The way to find out is to ask whether the barriers are continuous across the correlation.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'final', null, 13, 'dpFrontPosition returns 1 whenever the permeability ratio r is at or above 1. What does that encode?', '["That the formula is undefined for r above 1, so the engine substitutes its upper bound as a safe default.", "That a layer at least as permeable as the one currently breaking through has itself already broken through.", "That the layer is the one currently breaking through, and the ratio of a layer with itself is exactly 1.", "That layers of equal permeability are merged before the stage table is built, so r never exceeds 1 in practice."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The stages proceed from the most permeable layer downward, so at stage i any layer with k_j at or above k_i is already through and sits at x = 1 by definition rather than by clamping. The formula is not undefined there, nothing is merged, and the case is more general than the layer being compared with itself, since ties are possible.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'final', null, 14, 'The uncertainty in a layered sweep prediction is dominated by which of its inputs?', '["The three structural assumptions, which both methods share, rather than the choice between the methods or the mobility ratio.", "The mobility ratio, whose factor of ten moves the coverage at first breakthrough by about 14 points.", "The choice between Dykstra-Parsons and Stiles, which differ by 1.4 coverage points at Ekene''s mobility ratio.", "The permeability variation V, which is fitted from as few as three points and carries enormous uncertainty."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Piston displacement, no crossflow and equal porosity are carried identically by both methods and their combined effect is uncertain by tens of percent, which swamps the 1.4 point method gap and the compressed mobility response. The number is enough for screening, ranking and design comparison, and not enough for a reserves booking, and saying which of those you are doing is the point.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'final', null, 15, 'The Craig correlation returns 1 at a mobility ratio of about 0.15 and is capped there. What does a capped value mean physically?', '["That the correlation has left its validity range, so the returned value carries no physical meaning at all.", "That the areal sweep and the vertical sweep have both saturated, so the pattern is fully flooded.", "That breakthrough does not occur, since a fully swept pattern has no path for water to reach the producer.", "A front stable enough that it sweeps essentially the whole pattern before any water arrives at all."]'::jsonb, 3, 'At a strongly favourable mobility ratio the displacing front is broad and stable rather than fingered, so it reaches the corners at about the same time as the direct line, which is the ideal a polymer flood chases. 0.15 is the lower end of the quoted validity range rather than beyond it. Vertical sweep is a separate calculation, and breakthrough still occurs at a swept fraction of 1.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'final', null, 16, 'The growth coefficient 0.2749 in the after-breakthrough correlation is specific to what?', '["The mobility ratio range the underlying experiments covered, so it must be refitted outside it.", "The ratio of pattern area to net thickness, which sets how quickly the swept region can extend laterally.", "The five-spot geometry; other patterns carry different coefficients.", "The displacement construction, since it converts pore volumes injected into a fraction of the pattern area."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Like the breakthrough correlation, this is a regression through model data for a particular pattern, and a different geometry has its own coefficient. A pattern with a coefficient of 0.15 instead would reach full areal sweep much later from the same breakthrough value. The coefficient does not depend on the mobility ratio, the aspect ratio or the displacement solution.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'final', null, 17, 'The lesson says the logarithm in the growth law is not arbitrary. What supports that?', '["Streamline geometry: the extra area reached lies along slower and slower paths, whose transit times grow roughly exponentially with distance from the direct line.", "Material balance, which requires the swept area to be proportional to the logarithm of the injected volume.", "The Buckley-Leverett solution, whose rarefaction behind the front is logarithmic in the pore volumes injected.", "The Craig correlation, whose exponential term in the mobility ratio inverts to a logarithm after breakthrough."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Additional injected water reaches further and further out along paths that are progressively slower, and in a doublet the streamline transit times grow roughly exponentially with distance from the direct line, so the swept area grows roughly as the logarithm of the volume. That is a geometric argument rather than a consequence of material balance, the displacement solution or the other correlation.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'final', null, 18, 'An areal sweep of 1 has been reached. How much of the oil in the pattern has been recovered?', '["All of it within the pattern area, since the water has now contacted every part of the pattern.", "Only ED times EV of it, since areal sweep is one of three efficiencies and the only one at its ceiling.", "ED of it, since the vertical sweep is already folded into the flooded volume the engine reports against.", "It cannot be said without the water oil ratio limit, since recovery is set by where the run was stopped."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Areal sweep of 1 says the water has contacted the whole pattern area. Within that area the displacement efficiency decides how much oil left the rock and the vertical sweep decides how much of the column was contacted at all, and both are well below 1. The engine''s own reported recovery factor is of the flooded volume with EV already applied, which is a different denominator from the pattern element.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'final', null, 19, 'ED, EA and EV are each owned by a different part of the curriculum. Which owns which?', '["SCAL owns ED and EV together, since both follow from the relative permeability curves, and this module owns EA.", "The forecast module owns all three, since it is where they are multiplied together into a recovery factor.", "SCAL owns ED, this module owns EA, and the layered sweep module owns EV.", "The Associate ledger owns EV through the voidage balance, SCAL owns ED, and this module owns EA."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Displacement efficiency comes from the relative permeability curves and the viscosities and is constructed in the SCAL and Displacement course, areal sweep from the pattern geometry and the mobility ratio here, and vertical sweep from the permeability distribution and the mobility ratio in the layered module. The forecast multiplies them rather than owning them, and the voidage ledger contains no sweep term at all.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'final', null, 20, 'Two calculations of the same physical quantity disagree by 4.565110161166218e-5, by 4 percent, and by 40 percent in three different cases. What does the magnitude tell you?', '["Forty six parts per million is a rounded constant, four percent is a different definition, and forty percent is a bug.", "All three are unit conversion errors, differing only in how many conversions were chained together.", "All three require the same investigation, since a disagreement of any size means one calculation is wrong.", "Only the largest is worth investigating, since the other two are inside any realistic input uncertainty."]'::jsonb, 0, 'When two calculations of the same thing disagree there are three possibilities: one is wrong, they compute different things, or the difference is unit conversion, and the magnitude is what discriminates. The first two matter and the third is noise. Distinguishing them takes five minutes and saves days, which is why the 46 parts per million on the pore volume was worth a lesson despite never mattering numerically.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'final', null, 21, 'What is the practical rule the pore volume lesson gives for mixed unit systems?', '["Carry one unit system through the calculation and convert once at the boundaries, recording which direction the conversion went.", "Work in metric throughout and convert only the final answer, since the metric constants are exact.", "Convert every quantity to field units on entry, since the correlations are all published in field units.", "Keep both systems in parallel and compare at each step, so that a divergence is caught where it arises."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The Ekene case has metric statics from geoscience and a field-unit forecast engine, so a conversion is unavoidable and the discipline is to do it once and record it. Converting back and forth repeatedly adds another 46 parts per million on each round trip through the rounded constant and the errors do not cancel. Two of the four constants involved are exact by definition and two are rounded.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'final', null, 22, 'The forecast''s default time step is 30.4375 days. What is that number?', '["The mean interval between the six-monthly pressure surveys, divided into monthly sub-steps.", "The average length of the 36 ledger periods, which the forecast matches so the two series align.", "A mean month: 365.25 divided by 12.", "A step chosen to make the design case''s breakthrough fall exactly on a step boundary at 639.1875 days."]'::jsonb, 2, '365.25 over 12 is 30.4375, a mean month including the leap year quarter, and it is a fixed default rather than anything derived from Ekene. The ledger works in real calendar months of varying length, so the two do not align exactly, and 639.1875 days is 21 steps of 30.4375 as a consequence of the default rather than as its purpose.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'final', null, 23, 'After breakthrough the engine computes Qi = Wi / (PV x EA) before interpolating the displacement profile. Why divide by PV x EA rather than by PV?', '["Because EA converts the injected volume from reservoir to pore volumes, which the Welge profile is tabulated in.", "Because the displacement solution applies inside the swept region only, and EA is the fraction of the pattern swept.", "Because the areal sweep has to be removed before the profile is interpolated and reapplied to the result afterwards.", "Because Wi is a cumulative and PV x EA is the instantaneous volume, so the ratio gives a rate rather than a fraction."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The Buckley-Leverett profile describes displacement in the rock the water has actually reached, so the pore volumes injected must be expressed per unit of SWEPT pore volume rather than per unit of pattern volume. Dividing by PV alone would understate the throughput and so understate the displacement efficiency. Both Wi and PV x EA are cumulative volumes, so the ratio is dimensionless.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'final', null, 24, 'After breakthrough the engine computes cumulative oil as Np_rb = PV x EA x ED x (1 - Swc). What is the (1 - Swc) factor doing?', '["Converting the displacement efficiency from a fraction of movable oil to a fraction of the total oil in place.", "Removing the water already produced, so that the cumulative counts oil only.", "Scaling the areal sweep to the fraction of the pattern that contains mobile fluid at all.", "Converting pore volume to oil volume, since connate water occupies part of the pore space."]'::jsonb, 3, 'PV x EA is the swept pore volume, of which the fraction 1 - Swc was oil at the start, and ED is the fraction of that oil displaced. The displacement efficiency is already defined as a fraction of the initial oil, so no further conversion of it is needed. Produced water is not subtracted from an oil cumulative, and the areal sweep needs no such scaling.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'final', null, 25, 'The forecast march has three stop tests. What are they?', '["The areal sweep reaching 1, the vertical coverage reaching 1, and the maximum number of steps.", "The surface water oil ratio reaching its limit, the displacement profile being exhausted, and the horizon.", "The oil rate falling below an economic minimum, the injection allocation running out, and the horizon.", "The water cut reaching 1, the cumulative oil ceasing to increase, and the mobility ratio leaving its valid range."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The run breaks if the surface water oil ratio has reached the limit, if the interpolation has run off the end of the Welge profile, or at the maximum years. The areal sweep reaching 1 is a normal event that the run continues past, and the observed-rate case shows that the horizon test is a real stopping mode rather than a formality.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'final', null, 26, 'Raising the water oil ratio limit from 25 to 50 moves the design case to 2952.4375 days with Np 1744591.1985350684 and a final water oil ratio of 52.33279393821437. What does the extra 730 days buy?', '["About 34807 extra stock tank barrels, roughly two percent, against more than doubling the water handled.", "Nothing measurable, since the areal sweep had already saturated before the water oil ratio reached 25.", "About 34807 extra barrels at no additional water cost at all, since the water rate is capped once EA reaches 1.", "A rise in the recovery factor of the pattern element from 0.2854276257199057 to about 0.57."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Cumulative oil moves from 1709784.4164781766 to 1744591.1985350684 stb, about two percent, while the final water oil ratio goes from 31.119000015950355 to 52.33279393821437, so the incremental barrels are bought at a steeply rising water cost. The recovery factor of the FLOODED volume moves from 0.5545614215589451 to 0.5658508556836996, and the element figure is roughly half of each.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'final', null, 27, 'The engine''s fill-up warning reads: initial free gas, injection first refills PV*Sgi with no production response. What does the phrase ''no production response'' signal?', '["That the produced volumes reported during the fill-up period should be treated as unreliable and discarded.", "That the gas produced during fill-up is not carried in the output, though the liquid volumes are.", "That while the reduced Wi is at or below zero the areal sweep, the cumulative oil and the production are all zero.", "That production resumes only once the areal sweep has recovered to its pre-fill-up value."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Wi = max(0, Wi_total - PV x Sgi), and while that is zero the downstream chain gives zero areal sweep, zero cumulative oil and nothing produced at all. The engine has no gas phase, so there are no gas volumes to carry, and the areal sweep starts from zero after fill-up rather than recovering to a previous value. The artefact appears in the step just AFTER fill-up ends.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'final', null, 28, 'The monotonicity clamp Np = max(Np, Np_previous) is described as the right trade for a screening tool. What is being traded?', '["A cheap fix for a known numerical artefact at the breakthrough transition, against the ability to see a genuine non-monotonicity if one occurred.", "Exact material balance, against a cumulative that is guaranteed to increase at every step of the march.", "Resolution of the breakthrough time, against a cumulative oil curve that is smooth through the transition.", "Accuracy of the displacement efficiency, against a recovery factor that stays inside its physical bounds."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The clamp addresses a small backward step where the pre- and post-breakthrough formulas meet, and its cost is that a real decrease would be hidden rather than surfaced. Material balance is unaffected, since the cumulative is computed rather than accumulated, and neither the breakthrough resolution nor the displacement efficiency is involved.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'final', null, 29, 'A model says 63.72 years and the field says 1.16 years. How should that be characterised?', '["As a parameter mismatch, since the mobility ratio and vertical sweep together account for a factor of two.", "As a measurement problem, since a breakthrough date depends on when water production was first reported.", "As a limitation of screening correlations, which are known to be unreliable by an order of magnitude.", "As two descriptions of different physical processes rather than a disagreement over a parameter value."]'::jsonb, 3, 'No plausible adjustment of any parameter in the forecast produces a 425 day breakthrough at 104.7 rb/d into an element of this size: the mobility ratio buys a quarter, the vertical sweep buys a half, and the pattern approximation is worth tens of percent. A factor of fifty is a structural violation, and because the assumptions are stated it can be identified as the one saying that injected water sweeps the pattern element.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'final', null, 30, 'What does the lesson give as the correct use of a simple model?', '["To bound the answer from one side, so that the true result is known to lie either above or below the model''s own.", "Not to predict, but to establish what would have to be true, and then to notice that it plainly is not.", "To provide a starting point for a simulator, whose history match then refines the screening estimate.", "To rank alternatives against each other, since systematic errors cancel when two cases are compared."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The forecast established that a 63.72 year breakthrough would follow if the water swept the element, and the field''s 425 days then says the antecedent is false. That is a use that a model agreeing with the data could not have delivered. Ranking and bounding are real uses of screening tools elsewhere, but neither is what produced the finding here.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'final', null, 31, 'Why would tuning the forecast until it matched the field have been the wrong move?', '["It would have produced parameters outside the published validity ranges of both correlations.", "It would have required the allocation matrix to be refitted, which is a judgement call rather than data.", "It would have hidden the structural violation, which was the single most useful finding in the tier.", "It would have taken the mobility ratio below 0.15, where the Craig correlation is capped at 1."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A model whose disagreement with data is large, structural and interpretable is worth more than one tuned until it matches, because the tuning absorbs into parameters exactly the fact that mattered. Whether the tuned parameters would have been out of range is beside the point, and no amount of mobility ratio adjustment closes a factor of fifty in any case.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'final', null, 32, 'The implied contacted volume of 164686.15596920124 rb, spread over the full area between the wells at 20 percent porosity, corresponds to what thickness?', '["About half a foot, against 84 feet of net pay.", "About 8 feet, which is close to the thinnest layer in the modelled column.", "About 22 feet, which is the thickness of the fastest layer in the modelled column.", "About 43 feet, roughly half the net pay, which matches the vertical coverage at first breakthrough."]'::jsonb, 0, 'One sixty-eighth of the element''s pore volume spread over the whole area is a very thin sheet, and expressing it that way is what makes the number concrete: the water travelled through something like half a foot of connected rock. It is not the fastest layer of the model column, which is 22 feet thick and would carry far more volume, and the vertical coverage is a different quantity entirely.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'final', null, 33, 'If most of the injected water is short-circuiting, why is increasing the injection rate the wrong response?', '["It would push the mobility ratio further from unity, worsening both the areal and the vertical sweep.", "It sends more water down the same path.", "It would exceed the formation parting pressure, fracturing the injector and creating a second thief path.", "It would raise the voidage replacement above the operator band, which the ledger already reports at 1.20 in the north."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A channel is a preferential path, and adding volume to a system with a preferential path adds volume to the path. The correct response is conformance control: profile modification, selective completion, shutting off the offending interval, or a gel treatment. Injection rate does not move the mobility ratio, and while fracturing is a real constraint it is not why the response is wrong here.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'final', null, 34, 'Which result would falsify the channelling interpretation of the Ekene water arrival?', '["A tracer from Ekene-2 returning at Ekene-6 slowly and broadly, with a long tail and a large recovered mass fraction.", "A tracer returning at Ekene-6 within days of injection, at a high peak concentration.", "A Chan late-time slope below the 0.4 threshold on Ekene-3, which shares the same injectors.", "An allocation matrix revision that gave Ekene-6 a larger share of both injectors'' water."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A slow, broad, high-recovery tracer return is the signature of matrix displacement, and it would make both the shape argument and the volumetric argument wrong. A fast sharp return would confirm channelling rather than falsify it, a larger allocated share moves the answer from 1.47 to 3.58 percent without changing the conclusion, and Ekene-3 is a different well. A hypothesis nothing could falsify is not a hypothesis.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'final', null, 35, 'Roughly seventy percent of the element''s oil remains at the economic limit, in three distinct places. Why does knowing which place matters?', '["Because only one of the three can be addressed at all with current technology, and the other two are permanently unrecoverable.", "Because the recovery factor must be restated against a different denominator for each of the three.", "Because the three call for completely different technologies, and screening starts by deciding which holds the oil.", "Because the economic limit would have to be revised upward before any of the three could be reached."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Residual oil in swept rock needs a change in the capillary number through surfactant or miscible solvent, unswept pattern area needs infill or pattern realignment, and unswept layers need polymer or mechanical conformance control. Three targets, three technologies, and at Ekene the channelling evidence points at the conformance target, which happens also to be the cheapest.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'final', null, 36, 'Capstone field 1 returns a clean round number. Why is that itself a check?', '["A round value confirms the permeabilities were entered to the precision the engine expects.", "The least squares fit returns round values only when the residuals are at machine precision throughout.", "A different plotting position convention would give something untidy in the second decimal place.", "V is defined as a ratio of two tabulated permeabilities, so it is always a round number by construction."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The Ekene permeabilities were planted at the quantiles the engine''s own (i + 0.5)/n convention assigns, so recovering V = 0.5 exactly says the convention used matches the one the design assumed. Under i/n or i/(n+1) the same five permeabilities give something untidy. V is computed from a fitted sigma rather than read off two table entries.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'final', null, 37, 'A report states the flood''s VRR as 1.05 without further qualification. Which distinctions has it collapsed?', '["Reservoir volumes against surface volumes, which the formation volume factors relate by roughly twenty percent here.", "Instantaneous against rolling against cumulative, and field against pattern, which for Ekene span 0.61 to 1.24.", "Voidage replacement against fill-up, which are the same quantity measured before and after the crossing.", "Injected against allocated volume, which differ by the 12 percent that lands out of zone."]'::jsonb, 1, '1.05 is Ekene''s latest instantaneous field value, and the same flood reads 1.034899536109 cumulative, 1.2024353717815623 in the north element and 0.6097477559533482 in the south. All are correct and they answer different questions, which is why the qualifier is the number. VRR is a reservoir-volume ratio on both sides already, and fill-up is an event rather than a competing definition.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'final', null, 38, 'The Hall analysis on Ekene-4 gives a slope ratio of 1.0669369155108472 on absolute wellhead pressure and 1.4285714285714286 on pressure above the 2050 psia reference. Why does the convention matter so much here?', '["Against a 1.2 alert threshold, the absolute run raises no injectivity alert at all while the above-reference run raises exactly one.", "Because only the above-reference run is dimensionally consistent, so the absolute figure is simply wrong.", "Because the two conventions differ by the reference pressure of 2050 psia, a constant offset in the ratio.", "Because the absolute run integrates over a different time window, so the two are not comparable at all."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Same well, same month, same engine, and the pressure convention decides whether the problem is visible: 1.067 sits below the alert threshold and 1.4285714285714286 sits above it. Both runs are dimensionally fine and use the same window; the reference subtraction is not a constant offset in a ratio of fitted slopes. On pressure above the reference the slope IS 1 over the injectivity index, recovering the planted 0.5 to 0.35 step exactly.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'final', null, 39, 'The closing advice puts getting the ledger right first. Why does it come before everything else?', '["Because every analysis above it inherits its errors: rows in, rows out, wells by name, conventions stated.", "Because the ledger is the only calculation in the course whose result is actually graded at all three of the tiers.", "Because the pattern allocation cannot be validated until the field totals have been reconciled.", "Because the forecast takes its injection rate from the ledger, and no other input matters as much."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Pattern VRR, the allocation audit, the diagnostics and the reconciliation all sit on the period volumes and the well classification, so an error there propagates into every conclusion above without announcing itself. The allocation and the forecast are both examples of that dependence rather than the reason for it.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'final', null, 40, 'Why does the closing advice say to check the voidage proxy against the pressure measurement whenever a survey arrives?', '["Because the pressure survey is the only way to determine the formation volume factors that the ledger needs.", "Because the interpolated pressure track is more accurate than the closed-form model track.", "Because a structural disagreement there is worth more than any refinement of the ledger itself.", "Because the break-even VRR of 0.9869719699960521 can only be established from survey data."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The ledger predicts a pressure consequence and the survey measures one, so a mismatch says something the ledger cannot say about itself, which is more informative than making the ledger more precise. The interpolated survey series is coarser than the model track, missing the 2023-04 trough by 2.427063793101752 psi and reading it three months late, and the break-even value comes from the tank constants rather than from surveys.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'final', null, 41, 'Why does the closing advice say to split by geometry before drawing conclusions about wells?', '["Because per-well voidage is the only quantity the ledger cannot compute without an allocation matrix.", "Because the Chan diagnostic requires pattern membership before it can classify a producer''s water arrival.", "Because the pressure track is computed per pattern, so a field-level conclusion has no pressure to check against.", "Because a single field average can hide two separate elements that need corrections in opposite directions."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Ekene''s field cumulative of 1.034899536109 splits into elements at 1.2024353717815623 and 0.6097477559533482, one needing a cut and the other an increase, and the field number is not an approximation to either. The advice adds that the allocation must be carried with every conclusion that depends on it. Chan works from producer rates alone and the tank pressure is a field quantity.'),
  ('waterflood', 'advanced', 'final', null, 42, 'The tier''s three findings on Ekene are the voidage replacement, the pattern imbalance, and the swept fraction. What does the third add that the first two cannot?', '["That the pressure recovery of 34.4931292839633 psi was smaller than the ledger implied it should be.", "That the water in the over-injected element is not doing the displacement job the imbalance assumes it is doing.", "That the allocation matrix is wrong, since the northern element cannot have received the water it was credited with.", "That the injectivity loss at Ekene-4 is the mechanism behind both the imbalance and the early water arrival."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The first two findings are about volume and its distribution, and both are consistent with a flood that is working. The third says the northern water contacted at most 1.4697005138728763 percent of the element, which changes what the imbalance means and therefore what to do about it. The allocation is robust here by a wide margin, and Ekene-4 feeds the south more than the north.');
